(window.STEP_BOOK = window.STEP_BOOK || {})["L4"] = {"n":4,"level":"B2","t":"Inference, Attitude and Academic Listening","lessons":[{"id":"4.1","t":"Inference and Implied Meaning","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.1 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Listening Comprehension (20%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Infer what speakers mean but do not say directly.</p>\n<p>2. Interpret hints, indirect complaints, hesitation and tone.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يستنتج الطالب ما يقصده المتحدثون دون أن يصرّحوا به.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يفسّر التلميحات والشكاوى غير المباشرة والتردد والنبرة.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>At B2 level, many STEP listening questions test implied meaning. Speakers hint instead of saying things directly, especially when the message is negative or sensitive: Well, the food was … interesting (= not good). / I'm sure it's my fault, but I've waited forty minutes (= a complaint).</p>\n<p>Clues include hesitation (Um … well …), stress and tone, polite softeners (I was just wondering …, It's probably nothing, but …) and what the speaker chooses NOT to say.</p>\n<p>Typical stems: What does the man imply? What can be inferred about …? What does the woman mean when she says '…'? How does the student probably feel?</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ What does the man imply?</p>\n<p>▸ What can be inferred about the woman?</p>\n<p>▸ What does the speaker mean when she says '…'?</p>\n<p>▸ How does the student probably feel about …?</p>\n<p>▸ What will the man probably do?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في مستوى B2 يختبر كثير من أسئلة الاستماع المعنى الضمني؛ فالمتحدثون يلمّحون بدل التصريح خاصة في الرسائل السلبية أو الحساسة. ومن القرائن التردد والنبرة وعبارات التلطيف مثل I was just wondering وIt's probably nothing, but، وما يختار المتحدث ألا يقوله.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Ask: What does the speaker REALLY want or feel?</p>\n<p>2. Notice hesitation, softeners and pauses — they often signal a hidden negative message.</p>\n<p>3. Combine the words with the tone: positive words + flat or hesitant tone = probably negative.</p>\n<p>4. Choose a cautious option that the clues support (probably, may).</p>\n<p>5. Reject options that only repeat the speaker's words literally.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اسأل: ماذا يريد المتحدث أو يشعر حقًا؟ ولاحظ التردد وعبارات التلطيف والوقفات لأنها تدل غالبًا على رسالة سلبية خفية، واجمع بين الكلمات والنبرة، واختر بديلًا حذرًا تدعمه القرائن.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ The literal meaning of polite words.</p>\n<p>✗ An inference that goes much further than the clues.</p>\n<p>✗ A positive interpretation when the tone is negative.</p>\n<p>✗ Confusing what the speaker implies with what the other speaker says.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example recording: The New Haircut</strong> <em><button class=\"btn sm st-say\" data-tr=\"L-B2-01-EX\">▶ Listen</button></em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation:</strong> Two friends are talking. <em>(22 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Young man:</strong> So, what do you think of my new haircut?</p>\n<p><strong>Young man / boy:</strong> Um … well … it's very … different. I'm sure it'll grow quickly.</p>\n<p><strong>Young man:</strong> That bad, huh?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. What does the second boy imply?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. He loves the haircut.</p>\n<p>B. He doesn't really like the haircut.</p>\n<p>C. He wants the same haircut.</p>\n<p>D. He thinks the hair is too long.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Clues: hesitation (Um … well …), the vague word different, and I'm sure it'll grow quickly.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> His friend understands: That bad, huh?</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التردد وكلمة different ووعد بأن الشعر سينمو تدل على عدم الإعجاب دون تصريح.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B","quiz":[{"l":["Listen to each recording and answer the questions. You will hear each recording twice. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","استمع إلى كل تسجيل وأجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه. ستسمع كل تسجيل مرتين. اختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Recording 1 (Questions 1–6)"],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"Why does the man call the company?","o":["to arrange an interview","to ask about the result of his interview","to send his CV","to complain about the manager"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose] I just wanted to check on the status of my application.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":2,"s":"When was the man told he would hear back?","o":["by Friday","by Tuesday","next month","the same day"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] I was told I'd hear back by Friday.","h":"الموعد."},{"n":3,"s":"What does the woman imply?","o":["The man probably did not get the job.","The man got the job.","The man needs another interview.","The manager liked him most."],"k":0,"e":"[inference] some of them had a lot of experience … we'll keep your CV on file.","h":"الضمني."},{"n":4,"s":"Why does the woman mention that some applicants had a lot of experience?","o":["to praise the man's experience","to suggest gently that others were stronger","to ask him for more information","to explain the company's history"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] and … well, some of them had a lot of experience.","h":"غرض الذكر."},{"n":5,"s":"What does the woman suggest about the future?","o":["He should apply again next week.","He should call the manager directly.","He may be considered for another position.","He should get more education."],"k":2,"e":"[inference] We often have other positions opening … especially in customer service.","h":"المستقبل."},{"n":6,"s":"How does the man probably feel at the end?","o":["delighted","angry and rude","disappointed but polite","confused about the job"],"k":2,"e":"[inference] I see … OK. Thank you for your time.","h":"شعوره."}],"a":"L-B2-01-01"},{"l":["Recording 2 (Questions 7–12)"],"q":[{"n":7,"s":"What does the second girl imply about the second section?","o":["It was the easiest part.","She didn't have time to start it.","She got full marks.","She did badly on it."],"k":3,"e":"[inference] I'm really glad the final exam is worth sixty percent.","h":"الضمني."},{"n":8,"s":"Why does she mention that the final exam is worth sixty percent?","o":["She thinks the final is too important.","She hopes to improve her overall grade.","She wants to complain about the system.","She has already passed."],"k":1,"e":"[inference] really glad the final exam is worth sixty percent.","h":"غرض الجملة."},{"n":9,"s":"What went wrong in her preparation?","o":["She studied too late at night.","She forgot her calculator.","She didn't study at all.","She studied the wrong chapter."],"k":3,"e":"[reason] I studied the wrong chapter.","h":"الخطأ."},{"n":10,"s":"Why does she suggest getting coffee?","o":["She is very tired.","She wants to celebrate.","She wants to meet the teacher.","She wants to stop thinking about the exam."],"k":3,"e":"[reason] I need to think about something else.","h":"السبب."},{"n":11,"s":"What does the first girl offer?","o":["to study together for the final","to lend her notes from the exam","to talk to the teacher","to retake the exam with her"],"k":0,"e":"[function] we can study together for the final.","h":"العرض."},{"n":12,"s":"What can be inferred about the first girl?","o":["She also did badly.","She doesn't like coffee.","She is good at chemistry.","She is the chemistry teacher."],"k":2,"e":"[inference] Chemistry is my best subject.","h":"استنتاج."}],"a":"L-B2-01-02"},{"l":["Recording 3 (Questions 13–18)"],"q":[{"n":13,"s":"What does the customer mean when he says the food was 'fine'?","o":["The food was excellent.","The food was too cheap.","He didn't eat the food.","He was not completely satisfied."],"k":3,"e":"[inference] Well, the food was … fine. But …","h":"المعنى الضمني."},{"n":14,"s":"What is the customer's main complaint?","o":["His food arrived later than another family's.","His food was cold.","His table was too small.","The bill was wrong."],"k":0,"e":"[detail] ordered after us and got their food twenty minutes before we did.","h":"الشكوى."},{"n":15,"s":"Why does the customer mention that he has been coming for years?","o":["to ask for a discount","to say he knows the manager","to explain he likes the food","to show that the service has become worse"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] it never used to be like this.","h":"غرض الذكر."},{"n":16,"s":"What reason does the manager give?","o":["The kitchen is closed.","They are short of staff tonight.","The chef is new.","The other family booked earlier."],"k":1,"e":"[reason] We're short of staff tonight.","h":"السبب."},{"n":17,"s":"What can be inferred about last month's visit?","o":["The customer complained loudly.","The customer was also unhappy but stayed quiet.","Everything was perfect.","The customer didn't order coffee."],"k":1,"e":"[inference] my coffee came cold too, but I didn't say anything.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":18,"s":"What does the manager offer?","o":["a free meal next time","a discount on the main dishes","to take the desserts off the bill","a new table"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] let me take the desserts off your bill.","h":"العرض."}],"a":"L-B2-01-03"},{"l":["Recording 4 (Questions 19–24)"],"q":[{"n":19,"s":"What is the man's real purpose in talking to his neighbour?","o":["to complain politely about the parking","to praise the neighbour's son","to ask to borrow the car","to invite the neighbour for coffee"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose] when it's parked in front of my gate, my wife finds it a bit difficult.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":20,"s":"Why does he start by praising the car?","o":["because he wants to buy one","because he is jealous","to ask where it was bought","to make his complaint sound less direct"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] very nice … lovely car. It's just that … well …","h":"استراتيجية التلطيف."},{"n":21,"s":"Who has a problem because of the parking?","o":["the man's son","Abu Fahad","the hospital","the man's wife"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] my wife finds it a bit difficult to get our car out.","h":"من المتضرر."},{"n":22,"s":"Why does she need to leave early?","o":["She drives the children to school.","She goes to the hospital at six.","She works at the airport.","She goes to the gym."],"k":1,"e":"[reason] She has to leave for the hospital at six.","h":"السبب."},{"n":23,"s":"What does the man imply when he says 'It's probably only a few centimetres'?","o":["He wants the neighbour to move the car without feeling bad.","The problem is not real.","He will move the gate.","He wants a bigger car."],"k":0,"e":"[inference] It's probably only a few centimetres. I'm sure it's not a big problem.","h":"المعنى الضمني."},{"n":24,"s":"What will Abu Fahad do?","o":["sell the car","build a new garage","park in front of the gate","park next to the palm tree"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] I'll park on the other side, next to the palm tree.","h":"القرار."}],"a":"L-B2-01-04"},{"l":["Recording 5 (Questions 25–30)"],"q":[{"n":25,"s":"How does the student first respond to the request?","o":["with excitement","with anger","with hesitation","by refusing clearly"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] Um … sure, I can … try. Although …","h":"الرد الأول."},{"n":26,"s":"What does the student imply?","o":["She wants to do the whole presentation.","She doesn't want to work with the group.","She would prefer a different section.","She has already finished the history section."],"k":2,"e":"[inference] I'm much better with numbers.","h":"الضمني."},{"n":27,"s":"Why does she mention her last project?","o":["to complain about her old group","to show she has experience with finance","to say she is too busy","to ask for more time"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] I did all the financial analysis in our last project.","h":"غرض الذكر."},{"n":28,"s":"How many slides is the history section?","o":["about five","about twenty","about ten","about fifteen"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] It's about ten slides.","h":"العدد."},{"n":29,"s":"What does the leader suggest?","o":["that the student do both sections","that Noura leave the group","that the student swap sections with Noura","that they change the topic"],"k":2,"e":"[function] Maybe you two could swap?","h":"الاقتراح."},{"n":30,"s":"How does the student probably feel at the end?","o":["relieved","worried","angry","bored"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] That would be great!","h":"شعورها."}],"a":"L-B2-01-05"}],"n":30},{"id":"4.2","t":"Attitude and Tone","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.2 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Listening Comprehension (20%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Identify a speaker's attitude: enthusiastic, sceptical, critical, sarcastic, disappointed, neutral.</p>\n<p>2. Use word choice, stress and intonation to interpret tone.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يحدد الطالب موقف المتحدث: متحمس، متشكك، ناقد، ساخر، محبط، محايد.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يستخدم اختيار الكلمات والنبر والتنغيم لتفسير النبرة.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>Attitude questions ask how a speaker feels about something: What is the woman's attitude towards the new app? How does the man feel about his trip? The answer is often a single adjective: enthusiastic, optimistic, sceptical, critical, indifferent, disappointed, impressed, sarcastic.</p>\n<p>In listening, tone is carried by the voice: a rising, energetic voice suggests enthusiasm; a flat voice suggests boredom or disappointment; stressed words show strong feeling. Sarcasm means saying the opposite of what you mean: Oh, great — another traffic jam (= this is terrible).</p>\n<p>Word choice also matters: amazing, fascinating, a breakthrough (positive); I'm not convinced, supposedly, so-called (sceptical); a complete waste, badly organized (critical).</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ What is the woman's attitude towards …?</p>\n<p>▸ How does the man feel about …?</p>\n<p>▸ Which word best describes the speaker's tone?</p>\n<p>▸ What does the man mean when he says, 'Oh, wonderful'?</p>\n<p>▸ How would you describe the scientist's view of …?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أسئلة الموقف تسأل كيف يشعر المتحدث تجاه شيء ما، والإجابة غالبًا صفة واحدة مثل متحمس أو متشكك أو ناقد أو محبط أو ساخر. وفي الاستماع تحمل النبرة الموقف: الصوت الحيوي يدل على الحماس، والصوت الرتيب على الملل أو الإحباط، والسخرية قول عكس المقصود مثل Oh, great — another traffic jam.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Listen for evaluative words (amazing, disappointing, so-called).</p>\n<p>2. Notice the voice: energy, stress, flatness, laughter, sighs.</p>\n<p>3. Check for sarcasm: positive words in a negative situation.</p>\n<p>4. Decide the direction (positive / negative / mixed), then the strength (pleased vs thrilled).</p>\n<p>5. Separate the speaker's attitude from the other speaker's.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استمع للكلمات المقيّمة، ولاحظ الصوت من حيوية ونبر ورتابة، وتحقق من السخرية (كلمات إيجابية في موقف سلبي)، وحدد الاتجاه ثم الدرجة، وافصل موقف المتحدث عن موقف الآخر.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Taking sarcastic words literally.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing an attitude that is too strong (furious instead of annoyed).</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing the other speaker's attitude.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing neutral when the speaker uses strong evaluative words.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example recording: Another Meeting</strong> <em><button class=\"btn sm st-say\" data-tr=\"L-B2-02-EX\">▶ Listen</button></em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation:</strong> Two colleagues are talking. <em>(26 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Man:</strong> The manager has called another meeting for four o'clock.</p>\n<p><strong>Woman:</strong> Oh, fantastic. That's exactly what I needed — our third meeting today, and I haven't started my report.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. What is the woman's attitude towards the meeting?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. enthusiastic B. annoyed C. grateful D. indifferent</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Positive words (fantastic, exactly what I needed) in a negative situation = sarcasm.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Third meeting, report not started → she is annoyed.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كلمات إيجابية في موقف سلبي تعني السخرية، فالمرأة منزعجة.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B","quiz":[{"l":["Listen to each recording and answer the questions. You will hear each recording twice. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","استمع إلى كل تسجيل وأجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه. ستسمع كل تسجيل مرتين. اختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Recording 1 (Questions 1–6)"],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the woman's attitude towards the exhibition?","o":["very enthusiastic","mildly interested","critical","indifferent"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone] I was blown away … the most exciting show I've seen in years.","h":"موقفها."},{"n":2,"s":"What is the man's general attitude?","o":["mostly critical","very enthusiastic","completely neutral","angry and rude"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone] I can't share your excitement … many of them felt rushed.","h":"موقفه."},{"n":3,"s":"What does the woman accept about the man's criticism?","o":["The pieces were rushed.","The artists are too young.","The first room was weak.","The lighting was poor."],"k":3,"e":"[opinion] The lighting, yes, I'll give you that.","h":"ما قبلته."},{"n":4,"s":"Why does the woman think the artists deserve encouragement?","o":["They are famous.","They worked for many years.","They are very young.","They used digital art."],"k":2,"e":"[reason] the artists are very young — most are under thirty.","h":"السبب."},{"n":5,"s":"What does the man think a national gallery should do?","o":["show the best work, not just the newest","show only traditional art","support only young artists","charge less for entry"],"k":0,"e":"[opinion] a national gallery should show the best work, not just the newest.","h":"رأيه."},{"n":6,"s":"What do both critics agree about?","o":["The lighting was excellent.","The painting of the Empty Quarter is beautiful.","All the pieces were rushed.","The exhibition is too expensive."],"k":1,"e":"[opinion] The large painting of the Empty Quarter at sunset is genuinely beautiful … at least we agree on that.","h":"الاتفاق."}],"a":"L-B2-02-01"},{"l":["Recording 2 (Questions 7–12)"],"q":[{"n":7,"s":"What does the man mean when he says 'Truly a pleasure'?","o":["He enjoyed his drive.","His drive was terrible.","He likes driving slowly.","He took a new car."],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone] Only an hour and a half to travel twelve kilometres.","h":"السخرية."},{"n":8,"s":"Which word best describes the man's tone about the road works?","o":["grateful","enthusiastic","sarcastic","neutral"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] those brilliant road works … Next year!","h":"النبرة."},{"n":9,"s":"How long did his journey take?","o":["twenty-five minutes","an hour and a half","twelve minutes","half an hour"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] Only an hour and a half to travel twelve kilometres.","h":"المدة."},{"n":10,"s":"When will the road works finish, according to the sign?","o":["next month","next week","tomorrow","next year"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] the sign says they'll finish next year.","h":"الموعد."},{"n":11,"s":"Why hasn't the man used the metro before?","o":["It is too expensive.","He thought it would be slower.","There is no station near him.","He doesn't like crowds."],"k":1,"e":"[reason] I always thought it would be slower.","h":"السبب."},{"n":12,"s":"What will the man probably do tomorrow?","o":["take the metro","drive earlier","sleep in the office","take a taxi"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] I'll try it tomorrow.","h":"الخطوة التالية."}],"a":"L-B2-02-02"},{"l":["Recording 3 (Questions 13–18)"],"q":[{"n":13,"s":"What is the scientist's attitude towards sending humans to Mars?","o":["sceptical","worried and negative","enthusiastic and optimistic","uninterested"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] absolutely achievable … the most exciting time.","h":"موقفه."},{"n":14,"s":"Within how many years does he think it can happen?","o":["ten","seven","twenty","fifty"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] within the next twenty years.","h":"المدة."},{"n":15,"s":"According to him, what has reduced costs?","o":["smaller spacecraft","cheaper fuel","international cooperation","reusable rockets"],"k":3,"e":"[reason] Today, they land back on Earth and fly again.","h":"السبب."},{"n":16,"s":"How does he respond to the question about dangers?","o":["He says there are no dangers.","He accepts them but says they are being solved.","He refuses to answer.","He says the mission should stop."],"k":1,"e":"[opinion] The dangers are real … But we're solving these problems one by one.","h":"رده."},{"n":17,"s":"What is his only worry?","o":["funding","radiation","the length of the journey","young people's interest"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] My only worry is funding.","h":"قلقه."},{"n":18,"s":"What gives him hope?","o":["Young people are fascinated by space.","Governments always pay.","Mars is close to Earth.","Rockets are getting faster."],"k":0,"e":"[detail] young people are fascinated by space, and that gives me hope.","h":"مصدر الأمل."}],"a":"L-B2-02-03"},{"l":["Recording 4 (Questions 19–24)"],"q":[{"n":19,"s":"What is the father's attitude towards the app?","o":["enthusiastic","grateful","sceptical","indifferent"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] I'm not convinced … The so-called instant feedback.","h":"موقفه."},{"n":20,"s":"What does the phrase 'so-called instant feedback' show?","o":["He thinks the feedback is very fast.","He doubts the feedback is really useful.","He doesn't know what feedback means.","He wants more feedback."],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone] so-called … just a list of right and wrong answers.","h":"دلالة العبارة."},{"n":21,"s":"What does the teacher say the app does for English?","o":["It highlights grammar mistakes and suggests corrections.","It gives a list of right and wrong answers.","It reads stories aloud.","It translates words into Arabic."],"k":0,"e":"[detail] it highlights grammar mistakes and suggests corrections.","h":"ميزة الإنجليزي."},{"n":22,"s":"What has the father noticed about his son?","o":["He doesn't use the app.","He uses it only for English.","He studies longer now.","He clicks until the answer is correct."],"k":3,"e":"[detail] my son now just clicks until the answer turns green.","h":"ملاحظته."},{"n":23,"s":"What change is planned for next term?","o":["removing the app","adding videos","using it only for maths","a limit of three attempts per question"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] limit the number of attempts to three per question.","h":"التغيير."},{"n":24,"s":"How does the father feel at the end?","o":["completely convinced","very angry","cautious — willing to wait and see","excited"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] Well, that might help. I'll wait and see.","h":"شعوره."}],"a":"L-B2-02-04"},{"l":["Recording 5 (Questions 25–30)"],"q":[{"n":25,"s":"How did the woman feel before the trip?","o":["worried","excited","bored","angry"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] You were so excited before you left.","h":"قبل الرحلة."},{"n":26,"s":"What is her attitude towards the trip now?","o":["delighted","indifferent","disappointed","furious"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] It was … OK, I suppose … I expected much more.","h":"موقفها."},{"n":27,"s":"Why couldn't they do the boat trip?","o":["The boat was full.","It was too expensive.","They were ill.","It rained most of the week."],"k":3,"e":"[reason] it rained for five of the seven days, so we couldn't do the boat trip.","h":"السبب."},{"n":28,"s":"What was wrong with the hotel room?","o":["It was too small.","It was dirty.","It faced a car park.","It had no air conditioning."],"k":2,"e":"[detail] our room faced a car park.","h":"مشكلة الغرفة."},{"n":29,"s":"What did she enjoy?","o":["the hotel breakfast","the boat trip","the weather","the food and the old mosques"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] The food … was delicious … and the old mosques were beautiful.","h":"ما أعجبها."},{"n":30,"s":"What lesson has she learned?","o":["to read reviews carefully","to travel only in summer","to never visit Istanbul again","to take more money"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] Next time I'll read the reviews more carefully.","h":"الدرس."}],"a":"L-B2-02-05"}],"n":30},{"id":"4.3","t":"Recognizing Paraphrase","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.3 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Listening Comprehension (20%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Match what speakers say with options that use different words.</p>\n<p>2. Reject options that copy words from the recording but change the meaning.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يطابق الطالب كلام المتحدثين مع بدائل تستخدم كلمات مختلفة.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يرفض البدائل التي تنسخ كلمات التسجيل لكنها تغيّر المعنى.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>In STEP listening, the correct option almost never repeats the speaker's exact words. Instead, it paraphrases them: The bridge can expand in summer heat becomes The bridge gets longer when it is hot.</p>\n<p>Wrong options often do the opposite: they repeat several words you heard (expand, summer, bridge) but change the relationship, the number or the meaning. This is called a word-match trap.</p>\n<p>To choose correctly, listen for the MEANING. While listening, think in simple words: What is the speaker's point?</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following best restates what the speaker says about …?</p>\n<p>▸ What does the speaker say about …?</p>\n<p>▸ According to the speaker, …</p>\n<p>▸ Which statement is closest to the speaker's view?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في الاستماع لا يكرر البديل الصحيح كلمات المتحدث حرفيًا بل يعيد صياغتها، أما البدائل الخاطئة فتكرر كلمات سمعتها لكنها تغيّر العلاقة أو الرقم أو المعنى، ويسمى ذلك فخ مطابقة الكلمات. فاستمع للمعنى وفكّر بكلمات بسيطة: ما فكرة المتحدث؟</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. After each important point, say it to yourself in simple words.</p>\n<p>2. Be suspicious of options that repeat many exact words from the recording.</p>\n<p>3. Check the relationship: cause, contrast, comparison, condition.</p>\n<p>4. Check numbers and degree words (most, some, rarely, more than).</p>\n<p>5. Choose the option that keeps the meaning, even if the words are different.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بعد كل نقطة مهمة قلها لنفسك بكلمات بسيطة، وكن حذرًا من البدائل التي تكرر كلمات كثيرة من التسجيل، وتحقق من العلاقة والأرقام وكلمات الدرجة، واختر البديل الذي يحافظ على المعنى.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Word-match options that repeat exact words but change the meaning.</p>\n<p>✗ Reversed relationships (A causes B → B causes A).</p>\n<p>✗ Changed degree (some → all, rarely → never).</p>\n<p>✗ An option that is true in general but not what the speaker said.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example recording: Sleep and Memory</strong> <em><button class=\"btn sm st-say\" data-tr=\"L-B2-03-EX\">▶ Listen</button></em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation:</strong> A psychologist is speaking on the radio. <em>(42 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Woman:</strong> Students often stay up late before an exam, but research shows that sleep actually helps the brain store what we learned during the day. So a good night's sleep may do more for your result than an extra two hours of reading.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. Which of the following best restates the speaker's point?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. Students should read for two extra hours.</p>\n<p>B. Sleeping well can help you remember what you studied.</p>\n<p>C. Exams should start later in the day.</p>\n<p>D. Students sleep too much before exams.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Speaker's point in simple words: sleep helps memory, so sleep before exams.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> A repeats words (two hours, reading) but reverses the advice.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البديل A يكرر كلمات من التسجيل لكنه يعكس النصيحة، والصحيح B لأنه يعيد صياغة الفكرة.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B","quiz":[{"l":["Listen to each recording and answer the questions. You will hear each recording twice. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","استمع إلى كل تسجيل وأجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه. ستسمع كل تسجيل مرتين. اختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Recording 1 (Questions 1–6)"],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"Which of the following best restates the engineer's main point at the start?","o":["Bridges are built only in summer.","Bridges change size slightly with temperature.","Hot weather makes bridges stronger.","Most bridges are made of wood."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase] they move … expand when they get hot and contract when they cool down.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":2,"s":"According to the engineer, how much can a long bridge change in length?","o":["about five metres","less than one centimetre","exactly one metre","more than half a metre"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] can be more than half a metre.","h":"المقدار."},{"n":3,"s":"What is the purpose of expansion joints?","o":["to make the bridge look modern","to slow down cars","to collect rainwater","to let the bridge change length without damage"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] allow the bridge to change length safely.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":4,"s":"Which statement is closest to what he says about wind?","o":["Bridges must never move in the wind.","Flexible bridges are safer than completely stiff ones.","Wind is the main cause of expansion.","Long bridges are closed when it is windy."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase] A bridge that cannot bend is more likely to break.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":5,"s":"Why does he mention the small bump drivers feel?","o":["to help listeners recognize expansion joints","to warn drivers about dangerous roads","to complain about poor building","to explain how cars work"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose of detail] You've probably driven over them and felt a small bump.","h":"غرض الذكر."},{"n":6,"s":"What does he say about bridges in Riyadh?","o":["They are never inspected.","They have no expansion joints.","They are checked once every ten years.","They are inspected at least once a year."],"k":3,"e":"[detail] checked at least once a year.","h":"التفصيلة."}],"a":"L-B2-03-01"},{"l":["Recording 2 (Questions 7–12)"],"q":[{"n":7,"s":"Which of the following best restates the speaker's view of fruit juice?","o":["It is completely unhealthy.","It is healthier than eating fruit.","It is not as healthy as many people think.","It contains no sugar."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] that's only partly true.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":8,"s":"How much sugar can a glass of orange juice contain?","o":["about two teaspoons","about seven teaspoons","about seventeen teaspoons","none"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] about seven teaspoons.","h":"الكمية."},{"n":9,"s":"Why is whole fruit better than juice, according to the speaker?","o":["It has more sugar.","It is cheaper.","It contains no vitamins.","Its fibre slows down the sugar and makes you feel full."],"k":3,"e":"[reason] the fibre slows down how quickly the sugar enters your blood, and it makes you feel full.","h":"السبب."},{"n":10,"s":"What does she recommend?","o":["mostly water and only a small glass of juice a day","three glasses of juice a day","sports drinks after every meal","no fruit at all"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] drink water most of the time, keep juice to one small glass a day.","h":"النصيحة."},{"n":11,"s":"Which statement is closest to what she says about 'no added sugar' labels?","o":["Those drinks are always sugar-free.","Those labels are illegal.","Those drinks can still contain a lot of sugar.","Those drinks have more vitamins."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] Many drinks labelled 'no added sugar' are still very sweet.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":12,"s":"When might someone need a sports drink?","o":["after exercising hard for over an hour","with every meal","before sleeping","when watching sport"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] unless you're exercising hard for more than an hour.","h":"الشرط."}],"a":"L-B2-03-02"},{"l":["Recording 3 (Questions 13–18)"],"q":[{"n":13,"s":"Why does the economist use the example of a sandwich?","o":["to complain about canteen prices","to make the idea of inflation easy to understand","to explain how sandwiches are made","to show that food is healthy"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose of detail] Let me explain inflation in a simple way. Imagine …","h":"غرض المثال."},{"n":14,"s":"Which of the following best restates his definition of inflation?","o":["Prices in general go up over time, so money is worth less.","Some products become better over time.","People earn more every year.","Banks keep money for a long time."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase] a general rise in prices over time … your money buys less.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":15,"s":"What does he say about a small amount of inflation?","o":["It is always dangerous.","It never happens.","It is normal and can be good for the economy.","It should be stopped completely."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] is normal and even healthy for an economy.","h":"رأيه."},{"n":16,"s":"Why can very fast inflation cause problems?","o":["People spend too little.","Banks close.","Salaries don't rise fast enough.","Food becomes healthier."],"k":2,"e":"[reason] people's salaries can't keep up, and families struggle.","h":"السبب."},{"n":17,"s":"How do central banks mainly try to control inflation?","o":["by changing interest rates","by printing more money","by lowering salaries","by closing shops"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] mainly by changing interest rates.","h":"الأداة."},{"n":18,"s":"Which statement is closest to what happens when interest rates rise?","o":["Prices rise faster because people borrow more.","Salaries go up immediately.","Borrowing costs more, so spending falls and price rises slow down.","Banks lend money for free."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] borrowing becomes more expensive, so people … spend less, and prices usually rise more slowly.","h":"إعادة صياغة."}],"a":"L-B2-03-03"},{"l":["Recording 4 (Questions 19–24)"],"q":[{"n":19,"s":"Which of the following best restates the historian's first point?","o":["The Silk Road was a large network of different routes.","The Silk Road was one long straight road.","The Silk Road was only for silk.","The Silk Road connected only China and Europe by sea."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase] was not one road at all. It was a huge network of land and sea routes.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":20,"s":"For how long was the network used?","o":["about five hundred years","fifteen years","about one hundred years","more than fifteen hundred years"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] for more than fifteen hundred years.","h":"المدة."},{"n":21,"s":"Which statement is closest to what he says about merchants?","o":["Most merchants travelled the whole distance.","Most merchants travelled only part of the way.","Merchants never sold their goods.","Merchants travelled only by sea."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase] Very few merchants travelled the whole distance.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":22,"s":"Why did prices increase along the routes?","o":["Taxes were very high in China.","Silk became rare.","Sailors charged for the winds.","Goods were sold from trader to trader at many stops."],"k":3,"e":"[reason] goods passed from trader to trader … their price increased at every stop.","h":"السبب."},{"n":23,"s":"How did Arab sailors travel between the Gulf, India and East Africa?","o":["by following the coast at night","by using the monsoon winds","by using steam ships","by using camels on land"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] Arab sailors used the monsoon winds.","h":"الوسيلة."},{"n":24,"s":"According to the historian, what was the most valuable thing that travelled?","o":["silk","knowledge and ideas","precious stones","horses"],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase] the most valuable thing … was not a product but knowledge.","h":"الأثمن."}],"a":"L-B2-03-04"},{"l":["Recording 5 (Questions 25–30)"],"q":[{"n":25,"s":"Which of the following best restates the pilot's answer about turbulence?","o":["It is the main cause of plane accidents.","It never happens on modern planes.","It is uncomfortable but almost never dangerous.","It only happens over the sea."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] very rarely … It can feel uncomfortable, but modern planes are built to handle …","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":26,"s":"What does the pilot compare turbulence to?","o":["a car on a rough road","a storm in the desert","a train going round a bend","waves on the sea"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] like waves on the sea.","h":"التشبيه."},{"n":27,"s":"What do pilots often do to avoid turbulence?","o":["fly faster","land at another airport","change the plane's height","turn off the engines"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] we often change our height to find smoother air.","h":"الإجراء."},{"n":28,"s":"Which place is NOT mentioned as a common place for turbulence?","o":["over large cities","over mountains","near thunderstorms","in jet streams"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] mountains, thunderstorms, jet streams — not cities.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":29,"s":"Which statement is closest to what he says about injuries?","o":["Most injuries are caused by weak planes.","Most injuries happen to people without seat belts.","Pilots are often injured.","Injuries happen only during landing."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase] Most injuries happen to people who are … without their belt.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":30,"s":"What is the pilot's advice?","o":["only wear your belt when the sign is on","stand up during turbulence","avoid flying over mountains","keep your seat belt on whenever you are seated"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] whenever you're in your seat, keep your belt loosely fastened.","h":"النصيحة."}],"a":"L-B2-03-05"}],"n":30},{"id":"4.4","t":"Interviews","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.4 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Listening Comprehension (20%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Follow interviews with an interviewer and a guest.</p>\n<p>2. Answer a mixed set of questions on facts, opinions, reasons and attitudes in an interview.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يتابع الطالب المقابلات بين محاور وضيف.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يجيب عن مجموعة متنوعة من الأسئلة عن الحقائق والآراء والأسباب والمواقف في المقابلة.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>Interviews have a clear structure: the interviewer asks questions, and the guest answers, usually at length. The questions act as signposts — they tell you what the next part of the recording will be about.</p>\n<p>STEP interview questions are mixed: facts about the guest's life (When did she start?), reasons (Why did he choose …?), opinions and attitudes (How does she feel about …?) and advice (What does he recommend?).</p>\n<p>Remember that the answers come from the GUEST, not from the interviewer. The interviewer sometimes suggests an idea that the guest then rejects.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ What does the guest say about how she started?</p>\n<p>▸ Why did the man decide to …?</p>\n<p>▸ What is the woman's attitude towards …?</p>\n<p>▸ What advice does the guest give to young people?</p>\n<p>▸ What does the interviewer suggest that the guest disagrees with?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">للمقابلة بنية واضحة: يسأل المحاور ويجيب الضيف بإسهاب، وأسئلة المحاور إشارات لما سيأتي. وأسئلة الاختبار متنوعة: حقائق عن حياة الضيف، وأسباب، وآراء ومواقف، ونصائح. وتذكّر أن الإجابات تأتي من الضيف لا من المحاور، وقد يقترح المحاور فكرة يرفضها الضيف.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Use the interviewer's questions to divide the recording into sections.</p>\n<p>2. Match each STEP question to one section.</p>\n<p>3. Listen to the guest's full answer, especially after but or actually.</p>\n<p>4. Note whether the guest agrees or disagrees with the interviewer's suggestion.</p>\n<p>5. For advice questions, listen to the end of the interview.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استخدم أسئلة المحاور لتقسيم التسجيل إلى أجزاء، وطابق كل سؤال من أسئلة الاختبار مع جزء، واستمع لإجابة الضيف كاملة خاصة بعد but وactually، ولاحظ هل يوافق الضيف على اقتراح المحاور، واستمع للنهاية لمعرفة النصيحة.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ An idea suggested by the interviewer but rejected by the guest.</p>\n<p>✗ A fact from one part of the guest's life placed in another time.</p>\n<p>✗ The guest's past feeling instead of present feeling.</p>\n<p>✗ General advice that the guest does not actually give.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example recording: A Young Chef</strong> <em><button class=\"btn sm st-say\" data-tr=\"L-B2-04-EX\">▶ Listen</button></em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation:</strong> A radio interview. <em>(30 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Woman:</strong> You opened your restaurant at twenty-two. Was it your family's money?</p>\n<p><strong>Young man:</strong> Actually, no. People always assume that. I worked in a hotel kitchen for four years and saved every riyal.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. How did the chef pay for his restaurant?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. with his family's money B. with money he saved from working</p>\n<p>C. with a bank loan D. with a prize</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The interviewer suggests family money.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The guest says: Actually, no … saved every riyal.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اقتراح المحاور (مال العائلة) رفضه الضيف بكلمة Actually.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B","quiz":[{"l":["Listen to each recording and answer the questions. You will hear each recording twice. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","استمع إلى كل تسجيل وأجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه. ستسمع كل تسجيل مرتين. اختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Recording 1 (Questions 1–6)"],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"Where did the idea for the app come from?","o":["his mother's cooking","a university project","a business course","a restaurant he worked in"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] From my mother, actually.","h":"مصدر الفكرة."},{"n":2,"s":"What problem did the app solve?","o":["Restaurants were too expensive.","Delivery drivers needed work.","Home cooks had no easy, legal way to sell food.","Neighbours didn't like cooking."],"k":2,"e":"[reason] there was no easy way for home cooks to sell their food safely and legally.","h":"المشكلة."},{"n":3,"s":"What did one investor tell him?","o":["to move to Jeddah","to find a business partner","to sell the app","to come back when he had a degree"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] One told me to come back when I had a university degree.","h":"قول المستثمر."},{"n":4,"s":"Where did his first investment come from?","o":["his mother","a government programme for young business owners","a bank loan","a university"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] my first investment came from a government programme.","h":"مصدر التمويل."},{"n":5,"s":"How many home cooks use the app now?","o":["more than three thousand","about three hundred","thirty thousand","about one thousand"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] more than three thousand home cooks.","h":"العدد."},{"n":6,"s":"Which statement is closest to his advice?","o":["Wait until your idea is perfect.","Get a degree before starting.","Find investors before testing anything.","Begin with something small and test it, even if it isn't perfect."],"k":3,"e":"[paraphrase] Start small … Don't wait until everything is perfect.","h":"النصيحة."}],"a":"L-B2-04-01"},{"l":["Recording 2 (Questions 7–12)"],"q":[{"n":7,"s":"What does the coach say is most important for young players?","o":["winning every match","training every day","enjoying the game","having famous coaches"],"k":2,"e":"[opinion] more importantly, they enjoy it.","h":"الأهم."},{"n":8,"s":"Which idea of the interviewer's does the coach disagree with?","o":["The team trains hard.","Winning is everything at this age.","The team won the championship.","Children should have fun."],"k":1,"e":"[opinion] Some people say … winning is everything. — Not at all.","h":"الرفض."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following best restates the coach's view of winning?","o":["Winning is the only goal of training.","Winning is not possible for young teams.","Winning comes from improving; it is not the main aim.","Winning makes players stop loving football."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] winning is the result, not the goal. The goal is to improve every week.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the coach's rule about playing time?","o":["Only the best players play in finals.","Players change every ten minutes.","Every player plays at least half of every match.","Every player plays the whole match."],"k":2,"e":"[detail] every player on my team plays at least half of every match.","h":"القاعدة."},{"n":11,"s":"Why does he say 'I'd rather lose a match than lose a player'?","o":["He wants all players to keep loving football.","He doesn't care about winning.","He has too many players.","He thinks his team is weak."],"k":0,"e":"[inference] if a twelve-year-old sits on the bench … he'll stop loving football.","h":"غرض الجملة."},{"n":12,"s":"How does the coach feel about some boys training with an academy?","o":["worried","disappointed","surprised","very proud"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone] which makes me very proud.","h":"شعوره."}],"a":"L-B2-04-02"},{"l":["Recording 3 (Questions 13–18)"],"q":[{"n":13,"s":"According to Dr. Reem, what makes Red Sea corals special?","o":["They grow faster than other corals.","They can survive warmer water than most corals.","They are the most colourful in the world.","They live only in cold water."],"k":1,"e":"[detail] can survive temperatures two or three degrees higher.","h":"الميزة."},{"n":14,"s":"Why does she think they are so tough?","o":["They have adapted to warm water over thousands of years.","Scientists have protected them.","There is no pollution in the Red Sea.","Few people dive there."],"k":0,"e":"[reason] they have adapted over thousands of years to warm water.","h":"السبب."},{"n":15,"s":"How does she respond to the host's suggestion that the corals are safe?","o":["She disagrees and lists other threats.","She agrees completely.","She says she doesn't know.","She changes the subject."],"k":0,"e":"[opinion] I wouldn't say safe … they still face pollution, overfishing and damage.","h":"ردها."},{"n":16,"s":"How long does she usually spend underwater each day?","o":["about one hour","all day","about thirty minutes","about three hours"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] I spend about three hours underwater.","h":"المدة."},{"n":17,"s":"What is her attitude towards her job?","o":["She finds it hard but rewarding.","She finds it boring.","She wants to change jobs.","She finds it easy and relaxing."],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone] It's hard work, but every dive reminds me why I chose this job.","h":"موقفها."},{"n":18,"s":"What advice does she give to snorkellers?","o":["only snorkel in the north","collect pieces of coral","feed the fish","never touch or stand on coral"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] never touch or stand on coral.","h":"النصيحة."}],"a":"L-B2-04-03"},{"l":["Recording 4 (Questions 19–24)"],"q":[{"n":19,"s":"How long did the novel take to write?","o":["one year","almost five years","three years","fifteen months"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] Almost five years.","h":"المدة."},{"n":20,"s":"Why did she rewrite the book three times?","o":["Her publisher asked her to.","She changed the setting.","The three narrators sounded too similar.","She lost the first draft."],"k":2,"e":"[reason] all three sounded the same.","h":"السبب."},{"n":21,"s":"What does the interviewer wrongly assume?","o":["that the book is a bestseller","that she wrote quickly","that the narrators are men","that she grew up in Jeddah"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] Did you grow up there? — No, I grew up in Riyadh.","h":"الافتراض الخاطئ."},{"n":22,"s":"Where do many details in the book come from?","o":["history books","her grandmother's memories","old photographs","interviews with experts"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] Many details in the book come from her memories.","h":"المصدر."},{"n":23,"s":"Which statement is closest to her advice about first drafts?","o":["It is normal for a first draft to be poor.","A first draft should be perfect.","Never show your first draft to anyone.","First drafts should be written quickly in a week."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase] don't be afraid of a bad first draft. Every good book starts as a bad one.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":24,"s":"How long does she suggest writing each day?","o":["at least fifteen minutes","at least five hours","about one hour","only on weekends"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] write every day, even for fifteen minutes.","h":"المدة."}],"a":"L-B2-04-04"},{"l":["Recording 5 (Questions 25–30)"],"q":[{"n":25,"s":"What is Faisal's full-time job?","o":["firefighter","teacher","accountant","doctor"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] you're an accountant during the day.","h":"الوظيفة."},{"n":26,"s":"What made him want to volunteer?","o":["a friend's advice","seeing firefighters at a fire near his home","a TV programme","his job at the bank"],"k":1,"e":"[reason] there was a fire in a building near my house. I saw how hard the firefighters worked.","h":"السبب."},{"n":27,"s":"How long was the training?","o":["six months","four weeks","one year","four months"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] It lasted four months.","h":"المدة."},{"n":28,"s":"Which of the following best restates his view of firefighting?","o":["It is mainly about being brave.","It is easy if you are fit.","It depends more on care and following rules than on bravery.","It needs no training."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] it's really about being careful and following procedures.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":29,"s":"What does he find hardest?","o":["fighting large fires","the physical training","working at night","speaking to families who have lost their homes"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] It's speaking to families who have lost their homes.","h":"الأصعب."},{"n":30,"s":"What does he mean by 'You'll learn more than you give'?","o":["Volunteers should give money.","Volunteers gain a lot from the experience.","Volunteers must study first.","Volunteering is a waste of time."],"k":1,"e":"[inference] You'll learn more than you give.","h":"المعنى."}],"a":"L-B2-04-05"}],"n":30},{"id":"4.5","t":"Academic Mini-Lectures","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.5 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Listening Comprehension (20%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Understand the main idea and organization of a short academic lecture.</p>\n<p>2. Follow signposts that show definitions, processes, examples and comparisons.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يفهم الطالب الفكرة الرئيسة وتنظيم محاضرة أكاديمية قصيرة.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يتابع العبارات الإرشادية التي تدل على التعريف والعمليات والأمثلة والمقارنات.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>Mini-lectures are short academic talks of about two minutes. Lecturers usually announce the topic, define key terms, explain a process or compare ideas, give examples and sometimes summarize.</p>\n<p>Signposts show the organization: Today we'll look at … (topic); X is defined as … / By X, we mean … (definition); The first stage … then … finally (process); For example … (example); In contrast … / Similarly … (comparison); To sum up … (summary).</p>\n<p>STEP asks: What is the lecture mainly about? How does the lecturer organize the information? Why does the lecturer mention …? What happens after …?</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ What is the lecture mainly about?</p>\n<p>▸ How is the information organized?</p>\n<p>▸ Why does the lecturer mention …?</p>\n<p>▸ According to the lecturer, what happens after …?</p>\n<p>▸ What does the lecturer say about …?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحاضرات القصيرة أحاديث أكاديمية مدتها دقيقتان تقريبًا، يعلن فيها المحاضر الموضوع ويعرّف المصطلحات ويشرح عملية أو يقارن ويعطي أمثلة وربما يلخص. والعبارات الإرشادية تكشف التنظيم: Today we'll look at للموضوع، وBy X we mean للتعريف، وThe first stage… then للعملية، وIn contrast للمقارنة.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Write the topic from the first two sentences.</p>\n<p>2. Draw a simple note structure: definition → stages / points → examples → summary.</p>\n<p>3. Use signposts to know when a new point starts.</p>\n<p>4. For organization questions, look at your notes: is it a process, a comparison, causes and effects, or a history?</p>\n<p>5. For 'Why does the lecturer mention …?', ask what point the example supports.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اكتب الموضوع من أول جملتين، وارسم هيكل ملاحظات بسيطًا، واستخدم العبارات الإرشادية لمعرفة بداية كل نقطة، وفي أسئلة التنظيم انظر إلى ملاحظاتك: عملية أم مقارنة أم أسباب ونتائج أم تاريخ؟ وفي سؤال سبب ذكر المثال اسأل: أي نقطة يدعم؟</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ An example chosen as the main idea.</p>\n<p>✗ Stages of a process in the wrong order.</p>\n<p>✗ A comparison point given to the wrong item.</p>\n<p>✗ An organization type that does not match (chronological for a process).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example recording: What Is Erosion?</strong> <em><button class=\"btn sm st-say\" data-tr=\"L-B2-05-EX\">▶ Listen</button></em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation:</strong> A geography lecturer is starting a class. <em>(44 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Lecturer (female):</strong> Today we'll look at erosion. By erosion, we mean the movement of rock and soil by water, wind or ice. Let's begin with water, which is the most powerful. Rivers, for example, carry tiny pieces of rock and slowly cut valleys into the land.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. Why does the lecturer mention rivers?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. to give an example of erosion by water</p>\n<p>B. to compare rivers and wind</p>\n<p>C. to explain how ice forms</p>\n<p>D. to describe a valley trip</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Point: water is the most powerful.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> For example → rivers cut valleys.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عبارة for example تربط الأنهار بالنقطة السابقة: التعرية بالماء.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 A","quiz":[{"l":["Listen to each recording and answer the questions. You will hear each recording twice. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","استمع إلى كل تسجيل وأجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه. ستسمع كل تسجيل مرتين. اختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Recording 1 (Questions 1–6)"],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the lecture mainly about?","o":["why snow falls in winter","how glaciers change the landscape","the history of Yosemite Valley","how rivers form lakes"],"k":1,"e":"[main idea] Today we're going to look at how glaciers shape the land.","h":"الموضوع."},{"n":2,"s":"How does a glacier form?","o":["Rivers freeze in winter.","More snow falls than melts over many years.","Ice moves down from the sea.","Rocks become very cold."],"k":1,"e":"[detail] more snow falls in winter than melts in summer, year after year.","h":"التكوّن."},{"n":3,"s":"What happens during plucking?","o":["Rocks scrape the ground smooth.","Snow presses into ice.","Glaciers melt into lakes.","Water freezes in cracks and pulls rock away."],"k":3,"e":"[detail] water melts into cracks … freezes again and pulls pieces of rock away.","h":"النتف."},{"n":4,"s":"Why does the lecturer compare rocks in the glacier to sandpaper?","o":["to show that rocks are soft","to describe plucking","to explain how abrasion smooths the ground","to explain why glaciers are cold"],"k":2,"e":"[purpose of detail] work like sandpaper, scraping and smoothing the ground.","h":"غرض التشبيه."},{"n":5,"s":"How is most of the lecture organized?","o":["as a story in time order about one glacier","as a comparison of glaciers and deserts","as a description of two processes and their effects","as a list of famous scientists"],"k":2,"e":"[organization] There are two main processes. The first … The second …","h":"التنظيم."},{"n":6,"s":"What does the lecturer say in her conclusion?","o":["Glaciers move slowly but have a huge effect.","Glaciers move very quickly.","Glaciers are disappearing.","Glaciers only exist in America."],"k":0,"e":"[main idea] although glaciers move only a few metres a year, their effect on the land is enormous.","h":"الخلاصة."}],"a":"L-B2-05-01"},{"l":["Recording 2 (Questions 7–12)"],"q":[{"n":7,"s":"What is the lecture mainly about?","o":["how to make a profit","the history of fans","the basic idea of supply and demand","government control of prices"],"k":2,"e":"[main idea] one of the most basic ideas in economics: supply and demand.","h":"الموضوع."},{"n":8,"s":"How does the lecturer define demand?","o":["how much producers want to sell","how much people want to buy at different prices","how much money people have","how many shops sell a product"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] Demand means how much of a product people want to buy at different prices.","h":"التعريف."},{"n":9,"s":"According to the lecturer, why do higher prices increase supply?","o":["People buy more.","Governments ask them to.","Products become cheaper to make.","Producers can make more profit."],"k":3,"e":"[reason] higher prices encourage producers to supply more, because they can make more profit.","h":"السبب."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the equilibrium price?","o":["the highest possible price","the price set by the government","the price during a heat wave","the price where supply and demand meet"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] The point where supply and demand meet is called the equilibrium price.","h":"التعريف."},{"n":11,"s":"Why does the lecturer talk about fans in a heat wave?","o":["to advertise a product","to describe the weather","to give an example of supply and demand changing","to explain how factories work"],"k":2,"e":"[purpose of detail] Let me give you an example …","h":"غرض المثال."},{"n":12,"s":"What happens a few weeks after the heat wave in the example?","o":["Prices keep rising.","Shops stop selling fans.","Demand disappears completely.","Prices fall because there are more fans."],"k":3,"e":"[sequence] there are plenty of fans in the shops, and prices fall again.","h":"النتيجة."}],"a":"L-B2-05-02"},{"l":["Recording 3 (Questions 13–18)"],"q":[{"n":13,"s":"What is the lecture mainly about?","o":["how the lungs work","how to keep the heart healthy","the size of the human heart","how blood moves through the heart"],"k":3,"e":"[main idea] we'll follow the path of blood through the heart.","h":"الموضوع."},{"n":14,"s":"How is the lecture mainly organized?","o":["as a comparison of two organs","as a step-by-step process","as a list of heart diseases","as a history of medicine"],"k":1,"e":"[organization] comes into … then passes … Next … From there …","h":"التنظيم."},{"n":15,"s":"Where does blood go after the right ventricle?","o":["to the lungs","to the left atrium","to the whole body","to the right atrium"],"k":0,"e":"[sequence] the right ventricle, which pumps it to the lungs.","h":"الخطوة."},{"n":16,"s":"Which chamber is the strongest?","o":["the right atrium","the right ventricle","the left ventricle","the left atrium"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] the left ventricle — the strongest chamber.","h":"الأقوى."},{"n":17,"s":"Why does the lecturer compare valves to one-way doors?","o":["to describe the size of the heart","to explain that blood cannot flow backwards","to explain where oxygen comes from","to show that the heart is a muscle"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose of detail] work like one-way doors, so blood can't flow backwards.","h":"غرض التشبيه."},{"n":18,"s":"What causes the sound of a heartbeat?","o":["blood entering the lungs","the heart muscle growing","valves closing","air in the chambers"],"k":2,"e":"[reason] the sound of your heartbeat is actually the sound of these valves closing.","h":"السبب."}],"a":"L-B2-05-03"},{"l":["Recording 4 (Questions 19–24)"],"q":[{"n":19,"s":"What is the lecture mainly about?","o":["the life of Johannes Gutenberg","Arabic calligraphy","the printing press and its effects","book prices in China"],"k":2,"e":"[main idea] Today's topic is the printing press and how it changed the world.","h":"الموضوع."},{"n":20,"s":"Why were books rare before the printing press in Europe?","o":["They were copied slowly by hand.","Few people wanted them.","Paper was not invented.","The church banned them."],"k":0,"e":"[reason] copied by hand … could take … more than a year.","h":"السبب."},{"n":21,"s":"What does the lecturer say about printing in China and Korea?","o":["It started after Gutenberg.","It used only wooden letters.","It existed there centuries earlier.","It was never used there."],"k":2,"e":"[detail] Printing itself was not new — in China and Korea, people had printed … for centuries.","h":"التفصيلة."},{"n":22,"s":"How is the second half of the lecture organized?","o":["as a list of causes","as a comparison of two inventors","as a step-by-step process","as a list of effects"],"k":3,"e":"[organization] What were the effects? First … Second … Third …","h":"التنظيم."},{"n":23,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as an effect of printing?","o":["Books became cheaper.","Universities were closed.","Scientific ideas spread faster.","Local languages were used more."],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] cheaper, ideas spread, local languages — not universities closed.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":24,"s":"According to the lecturer, why did printing spread later in the Arab world?","o":["Handwritten calligraphy was highly valued.","There was no paper.","Printing was too expensive.","People could not read."],"k":0,"e":"[reason] partly because handwritten calligraphy was so highly valued.","h":"السبب."}],"a":"L-B2-05-04"},{"l":["Recording 5 (Questions 25–30)"],"q":[{"n":25,"s":"What is the lecture mainly about?","o":["how sand dunes move and their shapes","the history of the Empty Quarter","how to drive in the desert","how trees grow in sand"],"k":0,"e":"[main idea] they move. Today I'll explain how.","h":"الموضوع."},{"n":26,"s":"What is saltation?","o":["the steep side of a dune","the movement of sand grains in small jumps","a type of desert wind","the shape of a star dune"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] carries them a short distance in small jumps. This movement is called saltation.","h":"التعريف."},{"n":27,"s":"What happens when sand grains reach the top of a dune?","o":["They stop moving.","They are carried into the sky.","They form a new dune behind.","They fall down the steep side."],"k":3,"e":"[sequence] When they reach the top, they fall down the steep side.","h":"الخطوة."},{"n":28,"s":"How far can some dunes in Arabia move in a year?","o":["about one metre","more than ten metres","more than one kilometre","about a hundred metres"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] Some dunes in Arabia move more than ten metres a year.","h":"المسافة."},{"n":29,"s":"What is the difference between crescent and star dunes?","o":["Crescent dunes form with wind from one direction; star dunes with changing winds.","Crescent dunes are taller.","Star dunes form only at night.","Crescent dunes don't move."],"k":0,"e":"[organization] Crescent dunes … one direction. Star dunes … wind changes direction.","h":"المقارنة."},{"n":30,"s":"Why do people plant trees or build fences near dunes?","o":["to slow the dunes and protect roads and farms","to make the desert green","to stop sandstorms completely","to mark the borders"],"k":0,"e":"[reason] Moving dunes can cover roads and farms, so … to slow them down.","h":"السبب."}],"a":"L-B2-05-05"}],"n":30},{"id":"4.6","t":"Note-Taking and Note Completion","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.6 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Listening Comprehension (20%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Take short, organized notes while listening to a talk.</p>\n<p>2. Use notes to complete summaries and answer detail questions accurately.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يدوّن الطالب ملاحظات قصيرة ومنظمة أثناء الاستماع إلى حديث.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يستخدم ملاحظاته لإكمال الملخصات والإجابة عن أسئلة التفاصيل بدقة.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>Good listeners do not try to write every word. They write key words only: numbers, names, dates, stages and causes, using short forms and symbols (→ leads to, ↑ increase, ↓ decrease, = means, e.g. example).</p>\n<p>STEP note-completion items show part of a student's notes with a gap: Stage 2: water vapour rises and ______. The missing word or phrase must fit the notes grammatically and match the MEANING of the recording.</p>\n<p>Organize notes by the speaker's signposts: a new heading for each main point, with details indented below it. Then you can find any detail quickly when you read the question.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ Complete the student's notes: ______.</p>\n<p>▸ Which word best completes the note?</p>\n<p>▸ According to the notes, what happens after …?</p>\n<p>▸ Which detail should the student add under …?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المستمع الجيد لا يكتب كل كلمة بل الكلمات المفتاحية فقط: الأرقام والأسماء والتواريخ والمراحل والأسباب، مستخدمًا الاختصارات والرموز. وأسئلة إكمال الملاحظات تعرض جزءًا من ملاحظات طالب فيه فراغ، ويجب أن يناسب البديل الملاحظات نحويًا ويطابق معنى التسجيل. ونظّم ملاحظاتك بحسب العبارات الإرشادية.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Before listening, read the notes and predict the type of word in each gap (number, noun, verb).</p>\n<p>2. Use symbols and short forms to write fast.</p>\n<p>3. Start a new line for each signpost (first, next, another reason).</p>\n<p>4. Check that your answer fits the grammar of the note.</p>\n<p>5. Check spelling-type traps: similar numbers (15 / 50) and similar words.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قبل الاستماع اقرأ الملاحظات وتوقّع نوع الكلمة في كل فراغ، واستخدم الرموز والاختصارات، وابدأ سطرًا جديدًا مع كل عبارة إرشادية، وتأكد من أن إجابتك تناسب نحو الملاحظة، واحذر الأرقام المتشابهة مثل 15 و50.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ A word that is heard but belongs to a different stage or heading.</p>\n<p>✗ Similar-sounding numbers (13 / 30, 15 / 50).</p>\n<p>✗ A word that fits the meaning but not the grammar of the note.</p>\n<p>✗ A detail mentioned as a contrast, not as the answer.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example recording: The Life of a Honeybee</strong> <em><button class=\"btn sm st-say\" data-tr=\"L-B2-06-EX\">▶ Listen</button></em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation:</strong> A biology teacher is speaking. <em>(36 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Lecturer (female):</strong> A worker bee lives only about six weeks in summer. For the first three weeks, she works inside the hive, cleaning and feeding the young. After that, she becomes a forager, flying out to collect nectar.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. Complete the student's notes: Worker bee — weeks 1–3: inside hive; after week 3: ______.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. cleans the hive B. collects nectar outside</p>\n<p>C. feeds the queen only D. stays in the hive</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Notes are organized by time.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> After that → becomes a forager, flying out to collect nectar.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عبارة After that تفصل المرحلتين، والصحيح B.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B","quiz":[{"l":["Listen to each recording and answer the questions. You will hear each recording twice. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","استمع إلى كل تسجيل وأجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه. ستسمع كل تسجيل مرتين. اختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Recording 1 (Questions 1–6)"],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Stage 1 — evaporation: sun heats water → ______.","o":["water vapour (an invisible gas)","clouds","rain or snow","groundwater"],"k":0,"e":"[note completion] it turns into water vapour, an invisible gas.","h":"المرحلة الأولى."},{"n":2,"s":"Complete the student's notes: ______ % of water vapour comes from the oceans.","o":["19","50","9","90"],"k":3,"e":"[note completion] About ninety percent.","h":"الرقم."},{"n":3,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Stage 2 — condensation: vapour rises and ______.","o":["cools into tiny drops of water","heats up and disappears","falls as hail","soaks into the ground"],"k":0,"e":"[note completion] it cools down and changes back into tiny drops.","h":"المرحلة الثانية."},{"n":4,"s":"According to the teacher, what decides whether precipitation falls as rain, snow or hail?","o":["the size of the ocean","the temperature","the time of day","the wind direction"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] depending on the temperature.","h":"الشرط."},{"n":5,"s":"Which detail should the student add under Stage 4?","o":["Water turns into an invisible gas.","Water can soak into the ground and become groundwater.","Drops form clouds.","Drops become too heavy and fall."],"k":1,"e":"[note completion] it soaks into the ground and becomes groundwater.","h":"المرحلة الرابعة."},{"n":6,"s":"Why is groundwater especially important in desert countries?","o":["It forms clouds.","It stops evaporation.","It supplies many farms and towns.","It is warmer than rain."],"k":2,"e":"[reason] it supplies many farms and towns.","h":"السبب."}],"a":"L-B2-06-01"},{"l":["Recording 2 (Questions 7–12)"],"q":[{"n":7,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Mesopotamia = 'land between the ______'.","o":["mountains","seas","cities","rivers"],"k":3,"e":"[note completion] 'the land between the rivers'.","h":"المعنى."},{"n":8,"s":"Complete the student's notes: First cities appeared about ______ years ago.","o":["500","5,000","50,000","3,750"],"k":1,"e":"[note completion] Around five thousand years ago.","h":"التاريخ."},{"n":9,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Reason 1 — spring floods → ______.","o":["dangerous cities","new writing systems","rich soil for farming","fewer farmers"],"k":2,"e":"[note completion] the rivers flooded every spring and left rich soil.","h":"السبب الأول."},{"n":10,"s":"According to the teacher, what happened when farmers produced extra food?","o":["All people became farmers.","The rivers stopped flooding.","Some people could do other jobs.","Trade ended."],"k":2,"e":"[sequence] not everyone had to be a farmer. Some people became builders, traders …","h":"النتيجة."},{"n":11,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Cuneiform — wedge-shaped marks on ______.","o":["wet clay tablets","paper","stone walls","wooden boards"],"k":0,"e":"[note completion] pressed wedge-shaped marks into wet clay tablets.","h":"التفصيلة."},{"n":12,"s":"What was writing first used for?","o":["writing stories","writing laws","teaching children","recording trade and taxes"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] writing was used mainly to record trade and taxes, not stories.","h":"الاستخدام الأول."}],"a":"L-B2-06-02"},{"l":["Recording 3 (Questions 13–18)"],"q":[{"n":13,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Battery parts — anode (−), cathode (+), ______ in between.","o":["electron","electrolyte","device","lithium"],"k":1,"e":"[note completion] between them a chemical called the electrolyte.","h":"الأجزاء."},{"n":14,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Electricity = flow of electrons from ______ to cathode.","o":["cathode","anode","electrolyte","charger"],"k":1,"e":"[note completion] pushes electrons from the anode through the device to the cathode.","h":"الاتجاه."},{"n":15,"s":"What is the difference between normal and rechargeable batteries?","o":["Only normal batteries contain chemicals.","Rechargeable batteries have no anode.","Normal batteries last longer.","Only rechargeable batteries can reverse the reaction."],"k":3,"e":"[detail] Rechargeable batteries … can reverse the reaction.","h":"الفرق."},{"n":16,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Phone battery loses ≈ ______ % capacity after ≈ 500 charges.","o":["50","80","20","5"],"k":2,"e":"[note completion] lose about twenty percent of their capacity after around five hundred full charges.","h":"الرقم."},{"n":17,"s":"According to the teacher, what is the biggest enemy of batteries?","o":["heat","cold","water","charging at night"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] Heat is their biggest enemy.","h":"العدو الأكبر."},{"n":18,"s":"Which advice should the student write in the notes?","o":["Let the battery run empty every day.","Keep the battery between 20 and 80 percent.","Charge the battery in a hot car.","Always keep the battery at 100 percent."],"k":1,"e":"[note completion] better to keep the battery between twenty and eighty percent.","h":"النصيحة."}],"a":"L-B2-06-03"},{"l":["Recording 4 (Questions 19–24)"],"q":[{"n":19,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Habit loop = cue → ______ → reward.","o":["feeling","routine","message","trigger"],"k":1,"e":"[note completion] First, there's a cue … Second, there's the routine … Third, there's the reward.","h":"الحلقة."},{"n":20,"s":"Which detail should the student add under 'cue'?","o":["the good feeling the brain gets","the behaviour itself","the result of the habit","a time, a place or a feeling that triggers behaviour"],"k":3,"e":"[note completion] something that triggers the behaviour, such as a time of day, a place or a feeling.","h":"التفصيلة."},{"n":21,"s":"In the lecturer's example, what is the reward?","o":["seeing a new message","feeling bored","checking the phone","taking a walk"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] you see a new message — the reward.","h":"المكافأة."},{"n":22,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Average time to form a habit (study) = ______ days.","o":["21","16","66","60"],"k":2,"e":"[note completion] the average was actually sixty-six days.","h":"الرقم."},{"n":23,"s":"Why does the lecturer say 'don't give up too early'?","o":["Habits form in exactly 21 days.","Habits can take months to form.","Bad habits disappear quickly.","The study was wrong."],"k":1,"e":"[reason] it varied from about three weeks to eight months.","h":"السبب."},{"n":24,"s":"According to the lecturer, what is the best way to change a bad habit?","o":["keep the cue and reward but change the routine","remove all cues","stop all rewards","fight the feeling of boredom"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] keep the cue and the reward and change the routine.","h":"الطريقة."}],"a":"L-B2-06-04"},{"l":["Recording 5 (Questions 25–30)"],"q":[{"n":25,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Writing = main decoration because Islamic art avoided ______.","o":["colours in books","the use of pottery","large titles","pictures of people in religious places"],"k":3,"e":"[note completion] Islamic art traditionally avoided pictures of people in religious places.","h":"السبب."},{"n":26,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Kufic — oldest; ______ with straight lines.","o":["rounded","joined","angular","tiny"],"k":2,"e":"[note completion] Kufic, which is angular, with straight lines and sharp corners.","h":"الكوفي."},{"n":27,"s":"Which style is most printed Arabic text based on?","o":["Kufic","Thuluth","Diwani","Naskh"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] most printed Arabic books today use a style based on Naskh.","h":"النسخ."},{"n":28,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Diwani — Ottoman official documents; hard to ______.","o":["copy or forge","read in mosques","write with a reed","decorate"],"k":0,"e":"[note completion] hard to copy, which made it more difficult to forge.","h":"الديواني."},{"n":29,"s":"What is a qalam?","o":["a pen made from cut reed","a style of writing","a type of paper","a calligraphy school"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] a pen made from cut reed, called a qalam.","h":"التعريف."},{"n":30,"s":"Complete the student's notes: ______ — added to UNESCO intangible heritage list.","o":["2012","1921","2021","2001"],"k":2,"e":"[note completion] In twenty twenty-one.","h":"التاريخ."}],"a":"L-B2-06-05"}],"n":30}],"sc":{"L-B2-01-01":["Situation: A job applicant is phoning a company after an interview. (175 words)","Young man: Good morning. This is Yousef Al-Amri. I had an interview for the marketing assistant position last Tuesday. I was told I'd hear back by Friday, so I just wanted to check on the status of my application.","Woman: Ah, yes, Mr. Al-Amri. Thank you for calling. Um … the manager hasn't finished making decisions yet. We had a very large number of applicants, and … well, some of them had a lot of experience.","Young man: I see. Is there anything else I can send to support my application?","Woman: That's kind of you to offer. Actually, we'll keep your CV on file. We often have other positions opening during the year, especially in customer service.","Young man: OK. Thank you for your time.","Woman: Thank you for your interest, and good luck.","Young man: Thank you. Could I ask one more question? If I'm not chosen this time, could the manager give me some advice about how to improve my interviews?","Woman: I'll pass your request on to her. She's usually happy to give short feedback by email. It may take a week or two, though."],"L-B2-01-02":["Situation: Two friends are talking after an exam. (158 words)","Young woman / girl: So, how did the chemistry exam go?","Woman: Well … the first section was fine. I knew all the definitions.","Young woman / girl: And the second section? The calculations?","Woman: Let's just say I'm really glad the final exam is worth sixty percent.","Young woman / girl: Oh no. Was it the questions about reactions?","Woman: Those, and the ones about gas pressure. I studied the wrong chapter. Anyway, there's nothing I can do now. Do you want to get some coffee? I need to think about something else.","Young woman / girl: Sure. And if you want, we can study together for the final. Chemistry is my best subject.","Woman: Really? That would help a lot. Maybe we can start next week.","Woman: Thanks. Honestly, I was so worried this morning that I couldn't eat breakfast. I'll feel better after a coffee and a sandwich.","Young woman / girl: Then let's go to the café near the library. They make great cheese sandwiches, and it's quiet there in the afternoon.","Woman: Great. Let's go before it gets too busy."],"L-B2-01-03":["Situation: A customer is speaking to a restaurant manager. (157 words)","Man: Excuse me, are you the manager?","Man: Yes, I am. Is everything all right, sir?","Man: Well, the food was … fine. But I couldn't help noticing that the family at the next table ordered after us and got their food twenty minutes before we did.","Man: I'm very sorry about that. We're short of staff tonight.","Man: I understand that can happen. It's just that I've been coming here for years, and it never used to be like this. Last month, my coffee came cold too, but I didn't say anything.","Man: I really appreciate you telling me. Please let me take the desserts off your bill, and next time, ask for me personally.","Man: That's kind of you. Thank you.","Man: And may I ask what you ordered? I'd like to check with the kitchen so we can understand what went wrong tonight.","Man: The mixed grill and two lemon juices. The grill was good, to be fair, but it was warm, not hot."],"L-B2-01-04":["Situation: Two neighbours are talking outside their houses. (161 words)","Older man: Good morning, Abu Fahad. I hope you're well. I see you've bought a new car — very nice.","Man: Thank you! My son bought it for me. It's quite big.","Older man: Yes, I noticed. It's a lovely car. It's just that … well, when it's parked in front of my gate, my wife finds it a bit difficult to get our car out in the morning. She has to leave for the hospital at six.","Man: Oh! I'm so sorry. I didn't realize it was blocking your gate.","Older man: Not at all, not at all. It's probably only a few centimetres. I'm sure it's not a big problem for you to move it a little.","Man: Of course. From tonight, I'll park on the other side, next to the palm tree.","Older man: Thank you, my friend. And congratulations again on the car — your son is very generous.","Man: He is. You know, he says it's the best car he's ever driven, but I'm still learning where all the buttons are!"],"L-B2-01-05":["Situation: A group leader is talking to a student about a project. (159 words)","Woman: Lama, for our presentation, could you do the section on the history of the company? It's about ten slides.","Young woman / girl: The history section? Um … sure, I can … try. Although … I've never really studied business history.","Woman: That's fine, you can find everything online. Is there a problem?","Young woman / girl: No, no … it's just that I'm much better with numbers. I did all the financial analysis in our last project.","Woman: Oh, I didn't know that. Noura is doing the finance section, but she said she's worried about it. Maybe you two could swap?","Young woman / girl: That would be great! I'll talk to her after class.","Woman: Good. And Lama, thank you for telling me. It's always better to choose work that matches our strengths.","Young woman / girl: I agree. I'll also find some good graphs for the finance section. I think they'll make our presentation much clearer.","Woman: Perfect. Let's meet on Sunday to put all the slides together.","Young woman / girl: Sunday is fine. I'll bring my laptop and some snacks for everyone."],"L-B2-01-EX":["Situation: Two friends are talking. (22 words)","Young man: So, what do you think of my new haircut?","Young man / boy: Um … well … it's very … different. I'm sure it'll grow quickly.","Young man: That bad, huh?"],"L-B2-02-01":["Situation: Two art critics are discussing an exhibition on a radio programme. (166 words)","Woman: I visited the new exhibition of modern Saudi art at the national gallery yesterday, and I have to say, I was blown away. The use of traditional Sadu patterns in the digital pieces is brilliant. It's the most exciting show I've seen in years.","Man: Really? I'm afraid I can't share your excitement. Some pieces were interesting, I admit, but many of them felt rushed. And the lighting was so poor that I could hardly see the colours in the second room.","Woman: The lighting, yes, I'll give you that. But the artists are very young — most are under thirty. I think they deserve some encouragement.","Man: Encouragement, yes. But a national gallery should show the best work, not just the newest. Still, I'd recommend the first room. The large painting of the Empty Quarter at sunset is genuinely beautiful.","Woman: Well, at least we agree on that.","Man: Yes, it's a fair point to end on. The exhibition runs until the end of May, so listeners can judge for themselves."],"L-B2-02-02":["Situation: Two colleagues are arriving at the office. (153 words)","Man: Morning, Saad. You look tired.","Young man: Oh, I'm fine. I just had the most wonderful drive to work. Only an hour and a half to travel twelve kilometres. Truly a pleasure.","Man: The new road works on King Fahd Road?","Young man: Yes, those brilliant road works. They've closed two lanes, and the sign says they'll finish next year. Next year! I'm thinking of buying a tent and sleeping in the office.","Man: Have you tried the metro? The Blue Line goes right past the end of your street.","Young man: Actually, no. I always thought it would be slower. How long does it take you?","Man: Twenty-five minutes from my door, and I can read on the way.","Young man: Twenty-five? All right, you win. I'll try it tomorrow.","Man: Good. And bring your book — the journey goes much faster when you're reading.","Young man: You know, I haven't finished a book in months. Maybe this road works disaster has a silver lining after all."],"L-B2-02-03":["Situation: A scientist is being interviewed on a science programme. (165 words)","Woman: Dr. Hassan, many people think sending humans to Mars is science fiction. How do you see it?","Man: Oh, I think it's absolutely achievable — and I'd say within the next twenty years. Honestly, this is the most exciting time to be a space scientist. Ten years ago, rockets were used once and then thrown away. Today, they land back on Earth and fly again. That has reduced costs enormously.","Woman: But what about the dangers? The journey takes around seven months.","Man: The dangers are real — radiation, for example, and the mental pressure of being so far from home. But we're solving these problems one by one. And just imagine the knowledge we'll gain! If we find signs of ancient life on Mars, it would change how we understand our own planet.","Woman: You sound very confident.","Man: I am. My only worry is funding. Governments sometimes lose interest. But young people are fascinated by space, and that gives me hope.","Woman: Thank you, Dr. Hassan. Your enthusiasm is certainly catching."],"L-B2-02-04":["Situation: A father is talking to a teacher at a parents' evening. (153 words)","Woman: Have you tried the new homework app, Mr. Al-Ghamdi? Students upload their work, and the app gives instant feedback.","Older man: Yes, my son showed me. I'm not convinced, to be honest. The so-called instant feedback is just a list of right and wrong answers. It doesn't explain anything.","Woman: That's true for the maths exercises. But for English, it highlights grammar mistakes and suggests corrections.","Older man: Perhaps. But I've noticed that my son now just clicks until the answer turns green. Is he really learning, or is he just guessing faster?","Woman: That's a fair question. We're planning to limit the number of attempts to three per question next term.","Older man: Well, that might help. I'll wait and see.","Woman: That's all we ask. And please let me know if you notice any change after the new rule. Parents' comments really help us improve.","Older man: I will. I just want him to understand the work, not only finish it quickly."],"L-B2-02-05":["Situation: Two friends are talking about a holiday. (157 words)","Young woman / girl: Welcome back! How was Istanbul? You were so excited before you left.","Woman: Hmm. It was … OK, I suppose.","Young woman / girl: Just OK? What happened?","Woman: Well, it rained for five of the seven days, so we couldn't do the boat trip. The hotel looked lovely in the photos, but our room faced a car park, and the breakfast was the same every day.","Young woman / girl: Oh no. Was there anything good?","Woman: The food in the restaurants was delicious, I'll admit, and the old mosques were beautiful. But honestly, I expected much more for the money we paid. Next time I'll read the reviews more carefully instead of just looking at the pictures.","Young woman / girl: That's a shame. Did you at least bring back some sweets?","Woman: Of course! Turkish delight with pistachios. Come over tonight and try some — that part of the trip was definitely a success.","Young woman / girl: I'll be there at eight. And next time, let's plan a trip together — I'll read all the reviews!"],"L-B2-02-EX":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking. (26 words)","Man: The manager has called another meeting for four o'clock.","Woman: Oh, fantastic. That's exactly what I needed — our third meeting today, and I haven't started my report."],"L-B2-03-01":["Situation: An engineer is speaking to university students. (180 words)","Man: Today I want to explain one thing that many people never notice about bridges: they move. Steel and concrete expand when they get hot and contract when they cool down. On a long bridge, the difference between a summer afternoon and a winter night can be more than half a metre. If engineers didn't plan for this, the bridge would crack. So we include expansion joints — small gaps, usually covered with metal plates — that allow the bridge to change length safely. You've probably driven over them and felt a small bump. Another challenge is wind. Very long bridges are designed to bend slightly in strong winds, rather than stay completely rigid. A bridge that cannot bend is more likely to break. Finally, every major bridge is inspected regularly. In Saudi Arabia, for example, the bridges in Riyadh are checked at least once a year, and sensors on some newer bridges send data to engineers every day.","Man: In our next session, we'll look at how engineers test new bridge designs using computer models before a single piece of steel is ordered."],"L-B2-03-02":["Situation: A nutritionist is speaking on a health programme. (167 words)","Woman: A lot of people believe that fruit juice is a healthy alternative to fizzy drinks. Unfortunately, that's only partly true. A glass of orange juice from the shop can contain almost as much sugar as a can of cola — about seven teaspoons. The difference is that the sugar in juice comes from fruit, and juice also contains some vitamins. But your body still treats a large amount of sugar in the same way. Whole fruit is a much better choice, because the fibre slows down how quickly the sugar enters your blood, and it makes you feel full. My advice is simple: drink water most of the time, keep juice to one small glass a day, and read the labels. Many drinks labelled 'no added sugar' are still very sweet. And be careful with sports drinks — unless you're exercising hard for more than an hour, you probably don't need them.","Woman: Next week, I'll talk about healthy snacks for children's lunch boxes, so please send us your questions."],"L-B2-03-03":["Situation: An economist is speaking to secondary school students. (172 words)","Man: Let me explain inflation in a simple way. Imagine that ten years ago, a sandwich at your school canteen cost five riyals. Today, the same sandwich costs eight. The sandwich hasn't changed, but your money buys less than before. That is inflation: a general rise in prices over time. A small amount of inflation — about two percent a year — is normal and even healthy for an economy, because it encourages people to spend and invest instead of keeping all their money at home. The problem comes when prices rise very fast. Then people's salaries can't keep up, and families struggle to buy basic things. Central banks try to control inflation, mainly by changing interest rates. When they raise interest rates, borrowing becomes more expensive, so people and companies spend less, and prices usually rise more slowly. So, next time you hear on the news that interest rates have gone up, you'll know why.","Man: In our next class, we'll look at how inflation affects students — for example, the price of books and transport."],"L-B2-03-04":["Situation: A historian is speaking in a documentary. (167 words)","Man: The Silk Road was not one road at all. It was a huge network of land and sea routes connecting China with the Middle East and Europe for more than fifteen hundred years. Silk was famous, of course, but it was only one of many goods. Spices, paper, glass, horses and precious stones all travelled along these routes. Very few merchants travelled the whole distance. Instead, goods passed from trader to trader, city to city, and their price increased at every stop. The Arabian Peninsula played an important role, especially in the sea trade. Arab sailors used the monsoon winds to sail between the Gulf, India and East Africa. Perhaps the most valuable thing that travelled along the Silk Road, however, was not a product but knowledge: ideas about mathematics, medicine, astronomy and papermaking moved in both directions and changed the world.","Man: In the next episode, we'll follow a single bale of silk on its long journey from a workshop in China to a market in Damascus."],"L-B2-03-05":["Situation: A pilot is answering passengers' questions in an online video. (167 words)","Young man: One question I get all the time is: is turbulence dangerous? The short answer is: very rarely. Turbulence is simply moving air — like waves on the sea. It can feel uncomfortable, but modern planes are built to handle forces much stronger than anything you'll normally experience. Pilots get weather reports before and during every flight, and we often change our height to find smoother air. Turbulence is most common over mountains, near thunderstorms and when flying through jet streams, which are fast rivers of wind high in the sky. The biggest risk to passengers is not the plane — it's not wearing a seat belt. Most injuries happen to people who are walking around or sitting without their belt when the plane moves suddenly. So my advice is: whenever you're in your seat, keep your belt loosely fastened, even if the sign is off.","Young man: And one more thing: if you feel nervous, remember that your pilots fly through turbulence almost every day, and we're completely calm about it."],"L-B2-03-EX":["Situation: A psychologist is speaking on the radio. (42 words)","Woman: Students often stay up late before an exam, but research shows that sleep actually helps the brain store what we learned during the day. So a good night's sleep may do more for your result than an extra two hours of reading."],"L-B2-04-01":["Situation: A radio presenter is interviewing a young entrepreneur. (168 words)","Woman: My guest today is Abdullah Al-Saeed, who started a food-delivery app for home cooks when he was only nineteen. Abdullah, where did the idea come from?","Young man: From my mother, actually. She makes amazing traditional dishes, and neighbours were always asking to buy them. But there was no easy way for home cooks to sell their food safely and legally. So I thought: why not build a platform for them?","Woman: Was it difficult to start such a business so young?","Young man: Very. Many investors didn't take me seriously. One told me to come back when I had a university degree. In the end, my first investment came from a government programme that supports young business owners.","Woman: And now?","Young man: We have more than three thousand home cooks on the app, mostly women, and we've just started in Jeddah and Dammam.","Woman: What advice would you give to young people with a business idea?","Young man: Start small and test your idea with real customers. Don't wait until everything is perfect — it never will be."],"L-B2-04-02":["Situation: A TV presenter is interviewing a youth football coach. (156 words)","Man: Coach Ibrahim, your under-fourteen team has just won the national championship. Congratulations! What's the secret?","Older man: Thank you. Honestly, there's no secret. The boys train hard, but more importantly, they enjoy it. When children have fun, they want to learn.","Man: Some people say that at this age, winning is everything. Do you agree?","Older man: Not at all. I tell my players that winning is the result, not the goal. The goal is to improve every week. In fact, every player on my team plays at least half of every match — even in the final.","Man: Even in the final? That's brave.","Older man: Maybe. But if a twelve-year-old sits on the bench for a whole season, he'll stop loving football. I'd rather lose a match than lose a player.","Man: What's next for the team?","Older man: Some of the boys have been invited to train with a professional club's academy, which makes me very proud. For the rest, we'll start again in September."],"L-B2-04-03":["Situation: A podcast host is interviewing a marine biologist. (171 words)","Woman: Dr. Reem, you study coral reefs in the Red Sea. What makes them special?","Woman: They're remarkably tough. In many parts of the world, corals are dying because the ocean is getting warmer. But some Red Sea corals can survive temperatures two or three degrees higher than corals elsewhere. We think it's because they have adapted over thousands of years to warm water in the south of the sea.","Woman: So they're safe from climate change?","Woman: I wouldn't say safe. They're more resistant, but they still face pollution, overfishing and damage from boats and divers. And if temperatures keep rising, even they will suffer.","Woman: What does a typical day look like for you?","Woman: I spend about three hours underwater, measuring the health of the coral and taking small samples. The rest of the day is in the laboratory. It's hard work, but every dive reminds me why I chose this job.","Woman: What can ordinary people do?","Woman: If you dive or snorkel, never touch or stand on coral. And choose seafood from sustainable sources."],"L-B2-04-04":["Situation: A TV presenter is interviewing a novelist. (165 words)","Man: Your new novel, The House of Rawashin, has been a bestseller since it came out. How long did it take to write?","Woman: Almost five years. The first draft took only one year, but I wasn't happy with it. I rewrote the whole book three times.","Man: Three times! Why?","Woman: The story is told by a grandmother, a mother and a daughter, and in the first draft, all three sounded the same. It took a long time to give each woman her own voice.","Man: The novel is set in old Jeddah. Did you grow up there?","Woman: No, I grew up in Riyadh, but my grandmother was from Al-Balad, and she told me stories about the old houses every summer. Many details in the book come from her memories.","Man: What do you say to young people who want to write?","Woman: Read a lot, and write every day, even for fifteen minutes. And don't be afraid of a bad first draft. Every good book starts as a bad one."],"L-B2-04-05":["Situation: A radio presenter is interviewing a volunteer firefighter. (155 words)","Woman: Faisal, you're an accountant during the day and a volunteer firefighter in your free time. How did that happen?","Man: About six years ago, there was a fire in a building near my house. I saw how hard the firefighters worked, and I felt I wanted to help in some way. I found out that the Civil Defence trains volunteers, so I applied.","Woman: Is the training difficult?","Man: Very. It lasted four months — physical fitness, first aid, how to use the equipment and, most importantly, how to stay calm. People think firefighting is about being brave, but it's really about being careful and following procedures.","Woman: What's the hardest part?","Man: Honestly, it's not the fires. It's speaking to families who have lost their homes. You have to be strong for them.","Woman: Would you recommend volunteering to others?","Man: Absolutely. You don't have to be a firefighter. Hospitals, schools and charities all need volunteers. You'll learn more than you give."],"L-B2-04-EX":["Situation: A radio interview. (30 words)","Woman: You opened your restaurant at twenty-two. Was it your family's money?","Young man: Actually, no. People always assume that. I worked in a hotel kitchen for four years and saved every riyal."],"L-B2-05-01":["Situation: A geography lecturer is speaking to students. (172 words)","Lecturer (female): Today we're going to look at how glaciers shape the land. A glacier is a huge, slow-moving river of ice. It forms when more snow falls in winter than melts in summer, year after year. Over time, the weight of the snow presses the lower layers into solid ice. Now, how does a glacier change the landscape? There are two main processes. The first is called plucking. As the glacier moves, water melts into cracks in the rock below, freezes again and pulls pieces of rock away. The second process is abrasion. The rocks frozen into the bottom of the glacier work like sandpaper, scraping and smoothing the ground. Over thousands of years, these processes turn narrow river valleys into wide U-shaped valleys. A famous example is Yosemite Valley in the United States. When glaciers melt, they also leave behind huge piles of rock and soil, which can form hills and even dams for lakes. So, although glaciers move only a few metres a year, their effect on the land is enormous."],"L-B2-05-02":["Situation: An economics lecturer is speaking to first-year students. (166 words)","Lecturer (male): Let's start with one of the most basic ideas in economics: supply and demand. Demand means how much of a product people want to buy at different prices. Supply means how much of it producers are willing to sell. Generally, when prices go up, demand goes down, because fewer people can or want to pay. At the same time, higher prices encourage producers to supply more, because they can make more profit. The point where supply and demand meet is called the equilibrium price. Let me give you an example. Imagine there's a heat wave, and suddenly everyone wants to buy fans. Demand rises quickly, but shops only have a limited number, so prices go up. Seeing this, factories produce more fans. A few weeks later, there are plenty of fans in the shops, and prices fall again. Of course, real markets are more complicated — governments, advertising and even fashion can affect them — but this simple model explains a lot of what we see every day."],"L-B2-05-03":["Situation: A biology lecturer is speaking to students. (159 words)","Lecturer (female): In today's session, we'll follow the path of blood through the heart. The heart is a muscle about the size of your fist, and it has four chambers: two upper ones, called atria, and two lower ones, called ventricles. Let's start on the right side. Blood that has travelled around the body and lost its oxygen comes into the right atrium. It then passes down into the right ventricle, which pumps it to the lungs. In the lungs, the blood picks up fresh oxygen. Next, this oxygen-rich blood returns to the heart, entering the left atrium. From there, it moves into the left ventricle — the strongest chamber — which pumps it out to the whole body. Valves between the chambers work like one-way doors, so blood can't flow backwards. The sound of your heartbeat is actually the sound of these valves closing. A healthy adult heart beats around seventy times a minute, pumping about five litres of blood every minute."],"L-B2-05-04":["Situation: A history lecturer is speaking to students. (160 words)","Lecturer (male): Today's topic is the printing press and how it changed the world. Before the fifteenth century, most books in Europe were copied by hand. A single Bible could take a skilled writer more than a year, so books were rare and very expensive. Printing itself was not new — in China and Korea, people had printed with wooden blocks and even metal type for centuries. But around fourteen fifty, in Germany, Johannes Gutenberg developed a press with metal letters that could be arranged and reused quickly. Within fifty years, printing presses had spread to more than two hundred cities, and millions of books had been produced. What were the effects? First, books became cheaper, so more people learned to read. Second, scientific ideas spread faster, because scholars could share the same text in many places. Third, the use of local languages grew, instead of Latin only. In the Arab world, printing spread later, partly because handwritten calligraphy was so highly valued."],"L-B2-05-05":["Situation: A geology lecturer is speaking to students. (165 words)","Lecturer (female): Anyone who has driven through the Empty Quarter knows that sand dunes are not fixed — they move. Today I'll explain how. It all starts with wind. When the wind blows strongly enough, it lifts grains of sand and carries them a short distance in small jumps. This movement is called saltation. The sand grains are pushed up the gentle side of the dune, which faces the wind. When they reach the top, they fall down the steep side, called the slip face. As this happens again and again, the whole dune slowly moves forward in the direction of the wind. Some dunes in Arabia move more than ten metres a year. Now, dunes come in different shapes. Crescent dunes, shaped like a new moon, form where the wind usually blows from one direction. Star dunes, with several arms, form where the wind changes direction. Moving dunes can cover roads and farms, so in some areas people plant trees or build fences to slow them down."],"L-B2-05-EX":["Situation: A geography lecturer is starting a class. (44 words)","Lecturer (female): Today we'll look at erosion. By erosion, we mean the movement of rock and soil by water, wind or ice. Let's begin with water, which is the most powerful. Rivers, for example, carry tiny pieces of rock and slowly cut valleys into the land."],"L-B2-06-01":["Situation: A science teacher is speaking to a class. (167 words)","Woman: Today we'll review the water cycle, so get your notebooks ready. The cycle has four main stages. Stage one is evaporation. The sun heats water in oceans, lakes and rivers, and it turns into water vapour, an invisible gas. About ninety percent of the water vapour in the air comes from the oceans. Stage two is condensation. As the vapour rises, it cools down and changes back into tiny drops of liquid water. Millions of these drops together form clouds. Stage three is precipitation. When the drops join together and become too heavy, they fall as rain, snow or hail, depending on the temperature. Stage four is collection. The water collects in oceans, lakes and rivers, or it soaks into the ground and becomes groundwater. In desert countries like ours, groundwater is extremely important, because it supplies many farms and towns. And then, of course, the whole cycle begins again. The water you drink today may once have been part of a cloud over the Indian Ocean."],"L-B2-06-02":["Situation: A history teacher is speaking to students. (167 words)","Older man: Please take notes, because this will be in your test. Mesopotamia means 'the land between the rivers'. The two rivers are the Tigris and the Euphrates, in what is now mainly Iraq. Around five thousand years ago, this area saw the birth of some of the world's first cities. Why there? First, the rivers flooded every spring and left rich soil, which was perfect for farming. Second, farmers learned to dig canals to bring water to their fields, so they could grow more food than they needed. With extra food, not everyone had to be a farmer. Some people became builders, traders, priests and teachers. The Sumerians, who lived in the south, also developed one of the first writing systems, called cuneiform. They pressed wedge-shaped marks into wet clay tablets. At first, writing was used mainly to record trade and taxes, not stories. Later, the Babylonians wrote one of the earliest sets of laws, the Code of Hammurabi, around three thousand seven hundred and fifty years ago."],"L-B2-06-03":["Situation: A physics teacher is speaking to a class. (163 words)","Man: Today's topic is batteries — how they work and how to look after them. Every battery has three main parts: a negative end, called the anode, a positive end, called the cathode, and between them a chemical called the electrolyte. When you connect the battery to a device, a chemical reaction pushes electrons from the anode through the device to the cathode. That flow of electrons is electricity. In a normal battery, like the ones in a TV remote, the reaction can only happen once. Rechargeable batteries, like the lithium-ion batteries in phones, can reverse the reaction when you plug them in. However, they don't last for ever. Most phone batteries lose about twenty percent of their capacity after around five hundred full charges. Heat is their biggest enemy, so never leave your phone in a hot car, especially in a Saudi summer. It's also better to keep the battery between twenty and eighty percent than to let it run completely empty every day."],"L-B2-06-04":["Situation: A psychology lecturer is speaking to students. (172 words)","Lecturer (male): Let's talk about habits — and why they're so hard to change. Researchers describe a habit as a loop with three parts. First, there's a cue: something that triggers the behaviour, such as a time of day, a place or a feeling. Second, there's the routine: the behaviour itself. Third, there's the reward: the good feeling the brain gets, which makes it want to repeat the loop. For example, you feel bored — that's the cue — you check your phone — the routine — and you see a new message — the reward. Now, how long does it take to form a new habit? Many people say twenty-one days, but one well-known study found that the average was actually sixty-six days, and it varied from about three weeks to eight months. So don't give up too early. The best way to change a bad habit is not to fight the cue, but to keep the cue and the reward and change the routine. If you feel bored, take a short walk instead of picking up your phone."],"L-B2-06-05":["Situation: An art lecturer is speaking to students. (173 words)","Man: Today's lecture is about Arabic calligraphy, one of the great art forms of the Islamic world. Calligraphy simply means beautiful writing. Because Islamic art traditionally avoided pictures of people in religious places, writing became the main form of decoration in mosques, on pottery and in books. There are several main styles. The oldest is Kufic, which is angular, with straight lines and sharp corners. It was used in early copies of the Quran. Later came Naskh, which is rounded and easy to read; most printed Arabic books today use a style based on Naskh. Thuluth is large and elegant, and it's often used for titles and on the walls of mosques. Finally, Diwani was developed by the Ottomans for official documents; its letters are close together and hard to copy, which made it more difficult to forge. Calligraphers use a pen made from cut reed, called a qalam, and they spend years learning the exact proportions of each letter. In twenty twenty-one, Arabic calligraphy was added to UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage."],"L-B2-06-EX":["Situation: A biology teacher is speaking. (36 words)","Lecturer (female): A worker bee lives only about six weeks in summer. For the first three weeks, she works inside the hive, cleaning and feeding the young. After that, she becomes a forager, flying out to collect nectar."],"L-B2-UR-01":["Situation: A radio presenter is interviewing an archaeologist working in AlUla. (181 words)","Woman: Dr. Turki, you've been excavating in AlUla for eight years. What are you looking for?","Man: We're studying the Dadanite kingdom, which ruled the area more than two and a half thousand years ago — long before the Nabataeans built the famous tombs at Hegra. Most visitors know Hegra, but very few have heard of Dadan.","Woman: Why is that?","Man: Partly because the Nabataean tombs are so spectacular, and partly because Dadan was buried under sand for centuries. Until recently, we had only inscriptions and a few statues.","Woman: And what have you found?","Man: Last season, our team uncovered a temple and parts of what we believe was a market. We also found pottery from Egypt and Mesopotamia, which tells us Dadan was a major stop on the incense route.","Woman: That must have been exciting.","Man: Exciting, yes — but, honestly, most of archaeology is slow. We spend weeks brushing away sand, centimetre by centimetre. People who expect treasure every day soon get bored.","Woman: What would you like visitors to understand?","Man: That AlUla is not one story but many layers of history, one on top of another."],"L-B2-UR-02":["Situation: A geography lecturer is speaking to students. (178 words)","Lecturer (male): Today I want to introduce ocean currents — the great rivers that flow inside the sea. There are two main types. Surface currents, in the top few hundred metres, are driven mainly by wind. Deep currents are driven by differences in temperature and salt. Cold, salty water is heavier, so it sinks, especially near the poles, and slowly flows along the ocean floor. Warmer water moves in to replace it. Together, these movements create a global system that scientists call the ocean conveyor belt. A single drop of water may take up to a thousand years to complete the full journey. Why does this matter? First, currents move heat around the planet. The Gulf Stream, for example, carries warm water from the Caribbean towards Europe, which is why winters in Britain are milder than in parts of Canada at the same latitude. Second, currents carry nutrients that feed fish, so the richest fishing areas are often where deep water rises to the surface. Some scientists are worried that melting ice could weaken this system, with serious effects on weather."],"L-B2-UR-03":["Situation: Two friends are discussing which university to choose. (161 words)","Young woman / girl: So, have you decided? King Saud University or the new university in Abha?","Young man / boy: Not yet. Everyone says King Saud is the obvious choice — it's famous, it's in Riyadh, and my cousin studied there.","Young woman / girl: But?","Young man / boy: But the programme in Abha has something I really want: a full year of work placement in a real engineering company. At King Saud, it's only eight weeks.","Young woman / girl: That's a big difference. What about the cost of living?","Young man / boy: Abha's cheaper, and the weather's cooler, which my mother loves the idea of. The problem is that it's twelve hours' drive from home.","Young woman / girl: You could fly. It's only an hour and a half.","Young man / boy: True. And to be honest, being a bit far from home might be good for me. I've never lived on my own.","Young woman / girl: It sounds like you've already made up your mind.","Young man / boy: Maybe I have. I just didn't want to admit it.","Young woman / girl: Well, talk to your cousin. He might tell you what he wishes he'd done differently."],"L-B2-UR-04":["Situation: A school counsellor is giving a talk to students before exams. (170 words)","Woman: Good morning, everyone. With exams starting in three weeks, I want to talk about stress. First of all, a little stress is not your enemy. It can actually help you concentrate and work harder. The problem begins when stress stops you sleeping, eating or thinking clearly. So, here are three strategies that research supports. Number one: break big tasks into small ones. 'Revise biology' is frightening; 'read pages forty to fifty and make a mind map' is not. Number two: move your body. Even a twenty-minute walk reduces stress hormones and improves memory. And number three: protect your sleep. Students who sleep less than six hours before an exam often score lower than those who sleep eight, even if they studied more. Some of you might be thinking, 'That's easy for her to say.' And you're right — it's not always easy. But try one strategy this week, not all three at once. And remember, my door is open every day after the fourth lesson if you want to talk."],"L-B2-UR-05":["Situation: Two colleagues are discussing a new shopping app at work. (160 words)","Man: Have you seen the new version of our shopping app? The design team launched it yesterday.","Woman: I have. It looks beautiful, I'll give them that.","Man: I sense a 'but' coming.","Woman: Well, it took me four minutes to find the 'checkout' button. It's hidden under a menu now. If I struggled, imagine our older customers.","Man: Hmm. The designers said the clean look would attract younger users.","Woman: Maybe, but sales were down twelve percent yesterday compared with last Tuesday. That's not a coincidence.","Man: One day isn't enough data, though. Sales always change a bit.","Woman: Fair point. But we've also had more than three hundred complaints in the app store reviews.","Man: Three hundred? Wow. Then we need to tell the design team today, not next week.","Woman: I've already written a short report with screenshots. Could you look at it before I send it?","Man: Of course. Send it over, and let's suggest a quick fix: put the checkout button back on the main screen."],"L-B2-UR-06":["Situation: An astronomy lecturer is speaking to students. (168 words)","Lecturer (female): In this lecture, we'll follow the life cycle of a star. Every star begins in a nebula, a huge cloud of gas and dust. Gravity pulls the material together until the centre becomes hot enough for nuclear fusion to start. At that moment, a star is born. For most of its life, a star like our Sun burns hydrogen steadily; this is called the main sequence, and for the Sun it lasts about ten billion years. What happens next depends on the star's mass. A medium-sized star like the Sun swells into a red giant, then throws off its outer layers and leaves behind a small, hot core called a white dwarf. A much more massive star ends far more dramatically. It explodes as a supernova, one of the brightest events in the universe, and its core may become a neutron star or even a black hole. Interestingly, many of the elements in our bodies, such as calcium and iron, were made inside stars that exploded long ago."],"L-B2-UR-07":["Situation: A student is talking to her chemistry teacher after an experiment. (151 words)","Young woman / girl: Ms. Huda, can I talk to you about the experiment? Our results were completely different from everyone else's.","Woman: Of course, Lama. What happened?","Young woman / girl: We were supposed to measure how fast the reaction happens at different temperatures. The other groups found that it got faster when the water was hotter. But ours got slower.","Woman: Interesting. Did you check the thermometer?","Young woman / girl: Yes, it was fine. But now that I think about it, we used the acid from the second bottle, because the first one was nearly empty.","Woman: Ah. The second bottle is a weaker solution. I labelled it last week, but the label may have fallen off.","Young woman / girl: So it was the acid, not our method? I was so embarrassed in class.","Woman: Please don't be. Unexpected results are how science moves forward. Write up exactly what you did and suggest why your results were different. That's a better report than one with perfect results."],"L-B2-UR-08":["Situation: A museum guide is giving a talk about dhow boats. (164 words)","Older man: Welcome to the maritime section of the museum. The boat in front of you is a dhow, the traditional sailing ship of the Arabian Gulf and the Red Sea. Dhows have been used for at least two thousand years. Their most famous feature is the large triangular sail, called a lateen sail, which allowed sailors to travel even when the wind was not directly behind them. Traditionally, dhows were built without plans or drawings. Master builders worked from memory and experience, and the skills were passed from father to son. The planks were often made of teak wood brought from India, and in early dhows they were sewn together with coconut rope instead of nails. Dhows carried dates, pearls, spices and cloth between Arabia, India and East Africa, using the monsoon winds that change direction twice a year. Today, very few dhows are built by hand, but you can still see them in ports such as Jizan and in boat races during national celebrations."],"L-B2-UR-09":["Situation: A podcast host is interviewing a hospital volunteer. (175 words)","Man: Noura, you've volunteered at a children's hospital in Riyadh for three years. What exactly do you do?","Woman: I run a reading corner in the children's ward three afternoons a week. Some children stay in hospital for months, and they miss school and their friends. Reading together gives them something normal to look forward to.","Man: I imagine it can be emotionally difficult.","Woman: Sometimes, yes. But people expect it to be sad all the time, and it really isn't. Children are amazing — they laugh more than most adults I know.","Man: Did you need any training?","Woman: Yes, the hospital gave us two weeks of training on infection control and how to talk to sick children and their parents. You can't just walk in with a pile of books.","Man: What have you learned from the experience?","Woman: Patience, mostly. And that small things matter. One boy didn't speak to anyone for a week. Then one day he asked me to read the same dinosaur book for the fifth time. His mother cried. That was the best day of my year."]},"review":{"quiz":[{"l":["Unit Review — 50 questions on new recordings. You will hear each recording twice. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","مراجعة الوحدة: خمسون سؤالًا على تسجيلات جديدة، ستسمع كل تسجيل مرتين، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة.","Recording 1 (Questions 1–6)"],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the archaeologist mainly studying?","o":["the Nabataean tombs at Hegra","modern AlUla","Egyptian pottery workshops","the ancient Dadanite kingdom"],"k":3,"e":"[detail · 4.4] We're studying the Dadanite kingdom.","h":"موضوع الدراسة."},{"n":2,"s":"What can be inferred about the Dadanite kingdom?","o":["It existed before the Nabataeans built Hegra.","It was built by the Nabataeans.","It was famous among tourists.","It was found very recently by tourists."],"k":0,"e":"[inference · 4.1] long before the Nabataeans built the famous tombs at Hegra.","h":"الاستنتاج."},{"n":3,"s":"Which of the following best restates why few people know Dadan?","o":["Dadan was destroyed by an earthquake.","Dadan is closed to visitors.","There are no inscriptions about Dadan.","Hegra attracts more attention, and Dadan was covered by sand."],"k":3,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] Partly because the Nabataean tombs are so spectacular, and partly because Dadan was buried under sand.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":4,"s":"What does the pottery from Egypt and Mesopotamia suggest?","o":["Dadan made pottery for Egypt.","Dadan was an important trading stop.","Dadan was ruled by Egypt.","Dadan had no trade."],"k":1,"e":"[inference · 4.1] which tells us Dadan was a major stop on the incense route.","h":"الاستنتاج."},{"n":5,"s":"What is the archaeologist's attitude towards everyday work in archaeology?","o":["bored and wanting to stop","honest that it is slow and often not exciting","excited by treasure every day","angry about visitors"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] honestly, most of archaeology is slow … People who expect treasure every day soon get bored.","h":"الموقف."},{"n":6,"s":"What does he mean by 'many layers of history'?","o":["The tombs have many floors.","The sand is very deep.","Different civilizations lived there over time.","AlUla has many museums."],"k":2,"e":"[inference · 4.4] not one story but many layers of history, one on top of another.","h":"المعنى."}],"a":"L-B2-UR-01"},{"l":["Recording 2 (Questions 7–12)"],"q":[{"n":7,"s":"What is the lecture mainly about?","o":["how to fish in deep water","what ocean currents are and why they matter","the history of the Gulf Stream","why Britain has cold winters"],"k":1,"e":"[main idea · 4.5] introduce ocean currents … Why does this matter?","h":"الموضوع."},{"n":8,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Surface currents — driven mainly by ______.","o":["salt","wind","temperature","melting ice"],"k":1,"e":"[note completion · 4.6] Surface currents … are driven mainly by wind.","h":"الملاحظة."},{"n":9,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Deep currents — cold, salty water is heavier → ______.","o":["it rises","it freezes","it evaporates","it sinks"],"k":3,"e":"[note completion · 4.6] Cold, salty water is heavier, so it sinks.","h":"الملاحظة."},{"n":10,"s":"Why does the lecturer mention winters in Britain and Canada?","o":["to compare fishing in two countries","to show how currents carry heat","to explain how ice melts","to describe the latitude of Canada"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose of detail · 4.5] which is why winters in Britain are milder.","h":"غرض المثال."},{"n":11,"s":"Which statement is closest to what he says about fishing areas?","o":["Fish avoid deep water.","Fishing is best near melting ice.","The best fishing is often where deep water comes up.","Currents push fish away from coasts."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] the richest fishing areas are often where deep water rises to the surface.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":12,"s":"What is the lecturer's attitude towards the effect of melting ice?","o":["He reports some scientists' concern about it.","He is sure it will stop all currents tomorrow.","He thinks it is a joke.","He says it has no effect."],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] Some scientists are worried that melting ice could weaken this system.","h":"الموقف."}],"a":"L-B2-UR-02"},{"l":["Recording 3 (Questions 13–18)"],"q":[{"n":13,"s":"Why do people say King Saud is the obvious choice?","o":["It has a full-year placement.","It is the cheapest university.","It is famous, in Riyadh and his cousin went there.","It is near Abha."],"k":2,"e":"[reason · 4.4] it's famous, it's in Riyadh, and my cousin studied there.","h":"السبب."},{"n":14,"s":"What does the Abha programme offer that he really wants?","o":["eight weeks of placement","a full year of work placement","free accommodation","classes in Riyadh"],"k":1,"e":"[detail · 4.4] a full year of work placement in a real engineering company.","h":"التفصيلة."},{"n":15,"s":"Which of the following best restates his worry about Abha?","o":["It is very far from his family's home by car.","The weather there is too hot.","It is more expensive than Riyadh.","There are no flights there."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] it's twelve hours' drive from home.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":16,"s":"What does he imply when he says 'being a bit far from home might be good for me'?","o":["He doesn't like his family.","He wants to drive every weekend.","Living alone could help him become more independent.","He prefers King Saud."],"k":2,"e":"[inference · 4.1] I've never lived on my own.","h":"الاستنتاج."},{"n":17,"s":"What can be inferred about his decision?","o":["He will definitely choose King Saud.","He will not go to university.","He is probably going to choose Abha.","He will study abroad."],"k":2,"e":"[inference · 4.1] It sounds like you've already made up your mind. — Maybe I have.","h":"الاستنتاج."},{"n":18,"s":"What does the woman suggest at the end?","o":["visiting Abha first","asking his teachers","choosing the cheaper option","talking to his cousin about his experience"],"k":3,"e":"[detail · 4.4] talk to your cousin. He might tell you what he wishes he'd done differently.","h":"الاقتراح."}],"a":"L-B2-UR-03"},{"l":["Recording 4 (Questions 19–24)"],"q":[{"n":19,"s":"What is the counsellor's view of a little stress?","o":["It can be helpful.","It is always harmful.","It causes poor sleep.","It should be avoided completely."],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] a little stress is not your enemy. It can actually help you concentrate.","h":"الرأي."},{"n":20,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Strategy 1 — ______ big tasks into small ones.","o":["avoid","finish","write","break"],"k":3,"e":"[note completion · 4.6] break big tasks into small ones.","h":"الملاحظة."},{"n":21,"s":"Why does she compare 'Revise biology' with 'read pages forty to fifty'?","o":["to recommend biology as a subject","to show that small, specific tasks feel easier","to explain how to make a mind map","to show that reading is difficult"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose of detail · 4.5] 'Revise biology' is frightening; 'read pages forty to fifty …' is not.","h":"غرض المقارنة."},{"n":22,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Strategy 2 — a ______-minute walk reduces stress hormones.","o":["2","40","20","50"],"k":2,"e":"[note completion · 4.6] Even a twenty-minute walk.","h":"الرقم."},{"n":23,"s":"Which of the following best restates her point about sleep?","o":["Students should study all night.","Six hours of sleep is enough.","Sleeping well can matter more than extra study time.","Sleep has no effect on exam scores."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] who sleep less than six hours … often score lower … even if they studied more.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":24,"s":"What does she mean by 'my door is open'?","o":["Students can come and talk to her.","Her office has no door.","She leaves school early.","Students should open the door for her."],"k":0,"e":"[inference · 4.1] my door is open every day … if you want to talk.","h":"الاستنتاج."}],"a":"L-B2-UR-04"},{"l":["Recording 5 (Questions 25–30)"],"q":[{"n":25,"s":"What is the woman's attitude towards the new app at first?","o":["She loves everything about it.","She has not used it.","She thinks it is ugly and useless.","She likes its look but has serious concerns."],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] It looks beautiful, I'll give them that … but …","h":"الموقف."},{"n":26,"s":"What does the man mean by 'I sense a but coming'?","o":["He wants to change the subject.","He agrees with the designers.","He expects her to criticize it.","He doesn't understand her."],"k":2,"e":"[inference · 4.1] I sense a 'but' coming.","h":"الاستنتاج."},{"n":27,"s":"What problem did she have?","o":["The app would not open.","Prices were wrong.","The app was too slow.","She couldn't find the checkout button easily."],"k":3,"e":"[detail · 4.4] it took me four minutes to find the 'checkout' button.","h":"المشكلة."},{"n":28,"s":"Why does the man say one day isn't enough data?","o":["Sales naturally change from day to day.","The app has just closed.","He has not seen the figures.","Sales went up yesterday."],"k":0,"e":"[reason · 4.4] Sales always change a bit.","h":"السبب."},{"n":29,"s":"Which statement is closest to what changes the man's mind?","o":["the beautiful design","the twelve percent rise in sales","the large number of complaints","the opinion of younger users"],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] more than three hundred complaints … Then we need to tell the design team today.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":30,"s":"What do they plan to suggest?","o":["putting the checkout button back on the main screen","removing the app","making the design more colourful","waiting a week for more data"],"k":0,"e":"[detail · 4.4] put the checkout button back on the main screen.","h":"الاقتراح."}],"a":"L-B2-UR-05"},{"l":["Recording 6 (Questions 31–35)"],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"How is the lecture mainly organized?","o":["as stages in a life cycle, with two different endings","as a history of astronomers","as a list of planets","as a comparison of the Sun and the Moon"],"k":0,"e":"[organization · 4.5] begins … At that moment … next depends on the star's mass …","h":"التنظيم."},{"n":32,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Star begins in a ______ (cloud of gas and dust).","o":["supernova","nebula","white dwarf","red giant"],"k":1,"e":"[note completion · 4.6] Every star begins in a nebula.","h":"الملاحظة."},{"n":33,"s":"According to the lecturer, what decides how a star ends its life?","o":["its colour","its distance from Earth","its age","its mass"],"k":3,"e":"[detail · 4.5] What happens next depends on the star's mass.","h":"العامل."},{"n":34,"s":"Which of the following best restates what happens to a medium-sized star?","o":["It explodes and becomes a black hole.","It shrinks into a nebula.","It grows larger, loses its outer layers and becomes a small hot core.","It burns hydrogen for ever."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] swells into a red giant, then throws off its outer layers and leaves behind … a white dwarf.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":35,"s":"Why does the lecturer mention calcium and iron?","o":["to explain what stars eat","to describe the Sun's surface","to explain why black holes are dark","to show that our bodies contain material made in stars"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose of detail · 4.5] many of the elements in our bodies … were made inside stars.","h":"غرض الذكر."}],"a":"L-B2-UR-06"},{"l":["Recording 7 (Questions 36–40)"],"q":[{"n":36,"s":"Why does Lama want to talk to the teacher?","o":["Her group's results were different from the others'.","She broke a thermometer.","She wants to change groups.","She missed the experiment."],"k":0,"e":"[inference · 4.1] Our results were completely different from everyone else's.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":37,"s":"What did the other groups find?","o":["The reaction was faster in hotter water.","The reaction was slower in hotter water.","Temperature had no effect.","The acid was too strong."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] it got faster when the water was hotter.","h":"التفصيلة."},{"n":38,"s":"What can be inferred about the cause of the problem?","o":["The thermometer was broken.","Lama's group used a weaker acid by mistake.","Lama did not follow the method.","The water was too cold."],"k":1,"e":"[inference · 4.1] we used the acid from the second bottle … The second bottle is a weaker solution.","h":"الاستنتاج."},{"n":39,"s":"How did Lama feel in class?","o":["proud","bored","angry with the teacher","embarrassed"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] I was so embarrassed in class.","h":"شعورها."},{"n":40,"s":"What is the teacher's attitude towards the unexpected results?","o":["positive; she sees them as useful for learning","disappointed with the group","worried about safety","uninterested"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] Unexpected results are how science moves forward.","h":"موقفها."}],"a":"L-B2-UR-07"},{"l":["Recording 8 (Questions 41–45)"],"q":[{"n":41,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Dhows — used for at least ______ years.","o":["200","20,000","1,200","2,000"],"k":3,"e":"[note completion · 4.6] for at least two thousand years.","h":"الرقم."},{"n":42,"s":"What was the advantage of the lateen sail?","o":["It made boats faster in storms.","Sailors could travel when the wind was not behind them.","It was cheaper than other sails.","It allowed boats to carry more pearls."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] allowed sailors to travel even when the wind was not directly behind them.","h":"الفائدة."},{"n":43,"s":"What can be inferred about how dhow-building skills were learned?","o":["from books and drawings","at special universities","from Indian engineers","by working with experienced builders in the family"],"k":3,"e":"[inference · 4.1] Master builders worked from memory … passed from father to son.","h":"الاستنتاج."},{"n":44,"s":"Complete the student's notes: Early dhows — planks sewn together with ______.","o":["metal nails","teak wood","coconut rope","cloth"],"k":2,"e":"[note completion · 4.6] sewn together with coconut rope instead of nails.","h":"الملاحظة."},{"n":45,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cargo of dhows?","o":["gold","dates","pearls","spices"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 4.6] dates, pearls, spices and cloth — not gold.","h":"غير مذكور."}],"a":"L-B2-UR-08"},{"l":["Recording 9 (Questions 46–50)"],"q":[{"n":46,"s":"Why did Noura start the reading corner?","o":["to teach children to write","to give children in hospital something normal to enjoy","to sell books to parents","to train nurses"],"k":1,"e":"[inference · 4.1] Reading together gives them something normal to look forward to.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":47,"s":"How does Noura respond to the idea that the work is always sad?","o":["She agrees completely.","She disagrees and says the children laugh a lot.","She says she wants to stop.","She says the parents are sad."],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] people expect it to be sad all the time, and it really isn't.","h":"ردها."},{"n":48,"s":"What did her training include?","o":["first aid only","reading aloud and drama","infection control and talking to sick children","hospital management"],"k":2,"e":"[note completion · 4.6] training on infection control and how to talk to sick children and their parents.","h":"التدريب."},{"n":49,"s":"What does she mean by 'You can't just walk in with a pile of books'?","o":["Volunteers must be properly prepared first.","The hospital doesn't allow books.","Children don't like reading.","Books are too heavy to carry."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] the hospital gave us two weeks of training …","h":"المعنى."},{"n":50,"s":"Why does she tell the story about the boy and the dinosaur book?","o":["to recommend a book","to complain about the boy","to show that small moments can mean a lot","to explain why children read slowly"],"k":2,"e":"[purpose of detail · 4.4] small things matter … That was the best day of my year.","h":"غرض القصة."}],"a":"L-B2-UR-09"}],"n":50}};
